592186045585 true] [17592186138064 1069 "e-10171" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138064 1071 "Find a number in which the digit $5$ has a value that is $10$ times larger than the value of the digit $5$ in $57$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138064 1072 #uuid "45539eb4-9028-56ea-8d6d-23e3df125a76" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138064 1074 "Whenever you move a digit one space to the left in a place value chart, the value of the digit becomes ten times larger.\n\nLet's start by writing down the place values for $57$.\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{lightgray}{0}$ | $\\color{red}5$ | $\\color{lightgray}7$ |\n\nWe need to find a number in which the digit $\\color{blue}5$ has a value that is $10$ times larger than that of tens. So, we move $1$ step left in the place value chart:\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}5$ | $\\color{red}5$ |  |\n| ![](images/99/994dbc62d22b88ec4264c4696b322662b4af090a55d3f7f76fc2e6b6090c3a92.png) |  |  |\n\nTherefore, the required number is **any** number that has the digit $5$ in the hundreds place. For example, $\\bbox[2px,lightgray]{\\color{blue}5}12$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138065 1069 "e-10172" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138065 1071 "Find a number in which the digit $1$ has a value that is $1,000$ times larger than the value of the digit $1$ in $647,198$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138065 1072 #uuid "9eeba621-512f-5714-a3c0-9b87ffaa31ec" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138065 1074 "Whenever you move a digit one space to the left in a place value chart, the value of the digit becomes ten times larger.\n\nLet's start by writing down the place value chart for $647,198$.\n\n| **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{lightgray}6$ | $\\color{lightgray}4$ | $\\color{lightgray}7$ | $\\color{red}1$ | $\\color{lightgray}9$ | $\\color{lightgray}8$ |\n\nWe need to find a number in which the digit $\\color{blue}1$ has a value that is $1,000$ times larger than that of hundreds.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n1,000 = \\underbrace{10 \\times 10\\times 10}_{\\color{black}3\\,\\text{steps}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we move $3$ steps left in the place value chart:\n\n| **hundred-thousands** | **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}1$ |  |  | $\\color{red}1$ |  |  |\n| ![](images/c3/c3415abcf5dd7839902c2bc59c0b309830774b50be737d657e1d9a45a6e32496.png) |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, the required number is **any** number that has the digit $1$ in the hundred-thousands place. For example, $\\bbox[2px,lightgray]{\\color{blue}1}05,978$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138066 1069 "e-10175" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138066 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $702 > 1,024$\n2. $7,104 > 896$\n3. $11,359 > 8,937$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138066 1072 #uuid "91900615-9997-5a92-a3ad-4632e7f3df4d" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138066 1074 "Remember that \"$>$\" means \"greater than.\"\n\nWe can compare whole numbers with different numbers of digits by counting the digits. So, let's count the number of digits in each number.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\underbrace{702}_{\\color{blue}3\\,\\text{digits}}$ has fewer digits than $\\underbrace{1,024}_{\\color{blue}4\\,\\text{digits}}$, we conclude that $702 < 1,024$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\underbrace{7,104}_{\\color{blue}4\\,\\text{digits}}$ has more digits than $\\underbrace{896}_{\\color{blue}3\\,\\text{digits}}$, we conclude that $7,104 > 896$.\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\underbrace{11,359}_{\\color{blue}5\\,\\text{digits}}$ has more digits than $\\underbrace{8,937}_{\\color{blue}4\\,\\text{digits}}$, we conclude that $11,359 > 8,937$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138067 1069 "e-10233" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138067 1071 "Find a number in which the digit $6$ has a value that is $1,000$ times larger than the value of the digit $6$ in $3,465$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138067 1072 #uuid "a4a2f40e-8ace-54ea-b854-bd6a7882e953" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138067 1074 "Whenever you move a digit one space to the left in a place value chart, the value of the digit becomes ten times larger.\n\nLet's start by writing down the place value chart for $3,465$.\n\n| **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{lightgray}{0}$ | $\\color{lightgray}3$ | $\\color{lightgray}4$ | $\\color{red}6$ | $\\color{lightgray}5$ |\n\nWe need to find a number in which the digit $\\color{blue}6$ has a value that is $1,000$ times larger than that of tens.\n\nNotice that,\n\n$$\n1,000 = \\underbrace{10 \\times 10 \\times 10}_{\\color{black}3\\,\\text{steps}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we move $3$ steps left in the place value chart:\n\n| **ten-thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}6$ |  |  | $\\color{red}6$ |  |\n| ![](images/46/46f9db5a076554dd889a9ac188d0d8b00b037638261cb6aeaa20512aad664f9b.png) |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, the required number is *any* number with the digit $6$ in the ten-thousands place. For example, $\\bbox[2px,lightgray]{\\color{blue}6}5,291$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138068 1069 "e-10294" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138068 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $389 < 87$\n2. $2,100 < 58,162$\n3. $25,644 < 116,004$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138068 1072 #uuid "f46dc9d6-92c3-5204-ac7d-3d3fd575a09e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138068 1074 "Remember that \"$<$\" means \"less than.\"\n\nWe can compare whole numbers with different numbers of digits by counting the digits. So, let's count the number of digits in each number.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\underbrace{389}_{\\color{blue}3\\,\\text{digits}}$ has more digits than $\\underbrace{87}_{\\color{blue}2\\,\\text{digits}}$, we conclude that $389 > 87$.\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\underbrace{2,100}_{\\color{blue}4\\,\\text{digits}}$ has fewer digits than $\\underbrace{58,162}_{\\color{blue}5\\,\\text{digits}}$, we conclude that $2,100 < 58,162$.\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\underbrace{25,644}_{\\color{blue}5\\,\\text{digits}}$ has fewer digits than $\\underbrace{116,004}_{\\color{blue}6\\,\\text{digits}}$, we conclude that $25,644 < 116,004$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138069 1069 "e-10295" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138069 1071 "![](images/36/368132006382e550237a1c10198787bfef05bc37191cd508bdac0a550f030847.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a number line. Which of the following numbers could be placed into the empty box?\n\n1. $690$\n2. $72$\n3. $19,730$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138069 1072 #uuid "83e093a8-c5d9-59f7-8541-0f417f528dcc" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138069 1074 "Since the missing number lies to the *left* of $3,500$, it must be *less than* this number. Therefore,\n$0000$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our numbers in turn.\n\n- The number $690$ satisfies the condition since it has fewer digits than $3,500$.\n$3$\n- The number $72$ also satisfies the condition since it has fewer digits than $3,500$.\n$2$\n- The number $19,730$ does *not* satisfy the condition since it has more digits than $3,500$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138070 1069 "e-1153" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138070 1071 "What is the sum of the first and last terms of the following sequence?\n\n$3,\\quad-5,\\quad7,\\quad-9,\\quad11,\\quad-13,\\quad15$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138070 1072 #uuid "96401904-3d0f-56ac-ba31-eee3cbb016cb" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138070 1074 "The first term is $3$ and the last term is $15$. Their sum is\n\n$$\n3+15 = 18\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138071 1069 "e-1155" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138071 1071 "A sequence is defined as $a_n =n^2$ for $n\\geq 1$. What is the sum of its second and third terms?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138071 1072 #uuid "e9ccec31-a359-57b6-9817-9460a5b53d51" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138071 1074 "Using $a_n$ notation, the second and third terms are $a_2$ and $a_3$, respectively.\n\nWe can compute these values using the rule $a_n=n^2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2} &= 2^{2} = 4 \\\\\na_{3} &= 3^{2} = 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the second and third terms is\n\n$$\na_2+a_3=4+9=13\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138072 1069 "e-1156" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138072 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the sequence $f(n) =5-2n$ for $n\\geq 1$?\n\n1. Its second term is larger than the first term\n2. Its third term is $-1$\n3. Its last term is $-5$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138072 1072 #uuid "5060bada-52d2-5fef-b103-e375eb568c40" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138072 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- The first and second terms of the sequence are represented by $f(1)$ and $f(2)$, respectively. We can compute these values using the rule $f(n) = 5-2n$, as follows;\n$f(1)|= 5 - 2(1) = 3; f(2)|= 5 - 2(2) = 1$\nWe see that the second term $f(2)=1$ is smaller than the first term $f(1)=3$. Therefore, statement I is false.\n- We compute the third term as follows:\n$f(3)=5-2(3)=-1$\nTherefore, statement II is true.\n- The sequence is defined for $n\\geq 1$, which means that there is no limit to how large $n$ can be. There is no last term, because the sequence keeps going on forever. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138073 1069 "e-1181" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138073 1071 "An opera house has $92$ seats in the first row, $87$ in the second row, $82$ in the third row, and so on. If this trend continues, how many seats are in the $15$th row?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138073 1072 #uuid "bed158f8-b3b2-5ec6-ab8c-8f3936c420ce" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138073 1074 "The number of seats *decreases* by a fixed amount of $5$ seats per row. Therefore, the number of seats in the $n$th row can be modeled as an arithmetic sequence:\n\n$$\n92, \\: 87, \\: 82, \\: \\dots\n$$\n\nThe first term of the sequence is $a_1=92$, and the common difference is $d=-5$.\nSo, the $n$th term of this arithmetic sequence can be calculated using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&= 92 + (n - 1)(-5) \\\\\n&= 92 - 5n + 5 \\\\\n&= 97 - 5n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the number of seats in the $15$th row, we compute $a_{15}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{15} &= 97 - 5(15) \\\\\n&= 97 - 75 \\\\\n&= 22\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $15$th row has $22$ seats." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138074 1069 "e-1182" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138074 1071 "John decides to start a cycling training plan. On the first day, he rides $2\\,\\textrm{km}$, on the second day he rides $5\\,\\textrm{km}$, and on the third day, he rides $8\\,\\textrm{km}$. If this trend continues, on which day will he ride $29\\,\\textrm{km}$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138074 1072 #uuid "9d2c519e-a89e-5eb5-a2b8-a789a056ab83" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138074 1074 "John's cycling distance increases by a fixed amount of $3$ kilometers per day. Therefore, the number of kilometers John rides on the $n$th day can be modeled as an arithmetic sequence.\n\nWe are told that the first term is $a_1 = 2$, and the common diference is $d=3$. So, we can write the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&= 2 + (n - 1)(3) \\\\\n&= 2 + 3n - 3 \\\\\n&= 3n - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the day that John will ride $29\\,\\textrm{km}$, we calculate the value of $n$ such that $a_n=29$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 3n - 1 \\\\\n29 &= 3n - 1 \\\\\n30 &= 3n \\\\\n10 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, John will ride $29\\,\\textrm{km}$ on the $10$th day." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138075 1069 "e-12835" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138075 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{3}k +1 + \\dfrac{4}{3}k - 3$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138075 1072 #uuid "e85da1d1-1cf3-547c-86ce-42329475fbdd" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138075 1074 "We collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}k + 1 + \\frac{4}{3}k - 3 &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{3}k + \\frac{4}{3}k) + (1 - 3) &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{1 + 4}{3})k + (-2) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{3}k - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138076 1069 "e-12836" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138076 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{2}(p-4q)$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138076 1072 #uuid "30cf0891-33d3-566c-8286-4c161f84c1dd" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138076 1074 "Applying the distributive law and simplifying, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}(p - 4q) &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{2}) \\cdot p - (\\frac{1}{2}) \\cdot 4q &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}p - \\frac{4q}{2} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}p - 2q\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138077 1069 "e-12837" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138077 1071 "Simplify the expression $-\\dfrac{1}{2}u + \\dfrac{7}{8}u$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138077 1072 #uuid "53c1f7ca-bcaa-59e4-9929-0695b67621cb" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138077 1074 "We combine the like terms by adding the coefficients.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-\\frac{1}{2}u + \\frac{7}{8}u &=  \\\\\n&= (-\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{7}{8})u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to add the coefficients, we have to convert them to have the same denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{7}{8})u &=  \\\\\n(-\\frac{4}{8} + \\frac{7}{8})u &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{-4 + 7}{8})u &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{8}u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{8}u\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138078 1069 "e-12892" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138078 1071 "Simplify $\\dfrac{1}{3}(3a+6b)+2a$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138078 1072 #uuid "957b3102-6eb7-52d3-9299-54ed9bd4718e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138078 1074 "First, we apply the distributive law.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3}(3a + 6b) + 2a &=  \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{3}) \\cdot 3a + (\\frac{1}{3}) \\cdot 6b + 2a &=  \\\\\n\\frac{3a}{3} + \\frac{6b}{3} + 2a &=  \\\\\n&= a + 2b + 2a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + 2b + 2a &=  \\\\\na + 2a + 2b &=  \\\\\n&= 3a + 2b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138079 1069 "e-13011" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138079 1071 "Represent the inequality $x < -2$ or $x \\geq 1$ on a number line." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138079 1072 #uuid "014723fc-0b7d-5212-a41e-73847f5adb00" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138079 1074 "The inequality $x < -2$ or\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$\n\nmeans that $x$ can take any value less than $-2$, or greater than or equal to $1$.\n\nTo represent this on a number line, we graph the two separate inequalities:\n\n- the inequality $x < -2$ consists of an open circle at $-2$ and an arrow to the left.\n- the inequality $x \\geq 1$ consists of a closed circle at $1$ and an arrow to the right.\n\nThe resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/a1/a1067762a65fca32ca847a58dd47b07258f4ce58df20e45435df8a0a9e5d6f90.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138080 1069 "e-13012" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138080 1071 "![](images/b4/b4906893158bb5dc9f55363dcf2edc2ae1d30d8acff7121041d4a849ee73cbce.png)\n\nWhat inequality corresponds to the highlighted region shown on the number line above?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138080 1072 #uuid "21eace75-0b5a-5b37-903c-c238e3821bee" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138080 1074 "The red segment contains all values between $-2$ and $0$. In addition,\n\n- the closed circle at $-2$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $-2$, while\n- the open circle at $0$ implies that $x$ cannot be equal to $0$.\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq x < 0\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138081 1069 "e-13018" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138081 1071 "![](images/7a/7ae1311495d3fcb290bdf3f4b303224415e2e55c5ae1af8a82efd1f3aa2572b4.png)\n\nWhich inequality corresponds to the highlighted region shown on the number line above?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138081 1072 #uuid "e5b6a9dc-3a19-5496-9e22-d953ff4eb647" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138081 1074 "The red rays contain all values less than $-4$ or greater than $-1$. In addition,\n\n- the open circle at $-4$ implies that $x$ cannot be equal to $-4$, and\n- the closed circle at $-1$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $-1$.\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents $x < -4$ or\n\n$$\nx \\geq -1\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138082 1069 "e-13019" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138082 1071 "![](images/f7/f7a8d632deb19af6f7efc30182fb349f766c3ca7c10ba6567f9e2fd2e02e4b07.png)\n\nWhich inequality corresponds to the highlighted region shown on the number line above?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138082 1072 #uuid "347520ff-e759-5d99-acf7-0bc8a8f26689" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138082 1074 "The red segment contains all values between $-2$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nIn addition,\n\n- the closed circle at $-2$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $-2$, while\n- the closed circle at $\\dfrac32$ implies that $x$ can be equal to $\\dfrac32$.\n\nTherefore, the highlighted region represents\n\n$$\n-2 \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} \\leq} x \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} \\leq} \\dfrac32\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138083 1069 "e-13026" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138083 1071 "What is the solution to the equation $0.9s=3.6$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138083 1072 #uuid "6ff19222-3f56-53cf-bd39-b399573664b8" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138083 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10$ to get rid of the decimals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.9s &= 3.6 \\\\\n10 \\cdot (0.9s) &= 10 \\cdot (3.6) \\\\\n9s &= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the equation using the multiplication principle, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9s &= 36 \\\\\n\\frac{9s}{9} &= \\frac{36}{9} \\\\\n\\frac{9s}{9} &= \\frac{36}{9} \\\\\ns &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138084 1069 "e-13027" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138084 1071 "What is the solution to the equation $0.25s=0.5$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138084 1072 #uuid "015eac36-e18d-5a53-bfff-f301722a1f0c" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138084 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $100$ to get rid of the decimals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.25s &= 0.5 \\\\\n100 \\cdot (0.25s) &= 100 \\cdot (0.5) \\\\\n25s &= 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the equation using the multiplication principle, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25s &= 50 \\\\\n\\frac{25s}{25} &= \\frac{50}{25} \\\\\n\\frac{25s}{25} &= \\frac{50}{25} \\\\\ns &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138085 1069 "e-13028" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138085 1071 "If $0.25+0.03z=1$, then what is the value of $z$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138085 1072 #uuid "49ed1c21-223b-5dda-8f4d-7b6a3492a26b" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138085 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $100$ to get rid of the decimals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.25 + 0.03z &= 1 \\\\\n100 \\cdot (0.25 + 0.03z) &= 100 \\cdot (1) \\\\\n100 \\cdot (0.25) + 100 \\cdot (0.03z) &= 100 \\\\\n25 + 3z &= 100\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the equation using the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25 + 3z &= 100 \\\\\n3z + 25 &= 100 \\\\\n3z + 25 - 25 &= 100 - 25 \\\\\n3z &= 75 \\\\\n\\frac{3z}{3} &= \\frac{75}{3} \\\\\nz &= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138086 1069 "e-13045" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138086 1071 "What is the value of $\\mid 4\\!\\mid \\,$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138086 1072 #uuid "651f6d3a-6def-5064-b5c9-872d02a468fd" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138086 1074 "The absolute value of a positive number equals the number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\mid 4\\!\\mid \\, =4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of a number gives the distance between the number and zero on a number line.\n\n![](images/4d/4dc8f8174b1a226b3cfeaedc9e909adce1171c9daca1833d80ca09b286115755.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138087 1069 "e-13047" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138087 1071 "Find $\\mid {-2} \\mid$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138087 1072 #uuid "68b6b6aa-1346-5aaa-a909-fbd43513ba04" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138087 1074 "To find the absolute value of $-2$, we simply drop the negative sign:\n\n$$\n\\mid-2\\!\\mid=2\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of a number gives the distance between the number and zero on a number line.\n\n![](images/79/79429acd5f23eaca53c510a515096e419cbbf1f685868bfaa1bc8ee41498adce.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138088 1069 "e-13049" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138088 1071 "The point shown below represents a number on a number line. The absolute value of this number is $4$. What is the number?\n\n![](images/0e/0e8aaf68af47ecf3eadbd492af662273a68400bed20d3a5b214ec118abb6a2df.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138088 1072 #uuid "f8cde1b6-791d-5b26-be22-59d4cd59f7bf" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138088 1074 "The number is *negative* because it is to the *left* of zero.\n\nThe absolute value of the number is $4$, so the distance between that number and zero on a number line is $4$.\n\nThe negative number at a distance of $4$ from zero is $-4$, so the number is $-4$.\n\n![](images/b0/b0d99997e27059b23342de79fc887c85b56991e1312f430f80fcf39311479ad2.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138089 1069 "e-13129" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138089 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n${x + 2y = 8; 2x - y = 6$\n\nUse the diagram below to determine this system's solution $(x,y)$.\n\n![](images/6f/6f0b7960b9b4c98250741b009da2ad19c1123c5b7adf9cd5072430fb9a879dc9.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138089 1072 #uuid "cbf28a0c-c34d-5855-b72e-4225a11710c1" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138089 1074 "The solution of the system corresponds to the point of intersection of the lines.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1c69adc880ea7cfdb5a4e0a10abd6032d2eedf0386923b94df9b7c173edd6d.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(4,2)$. Therefore, the solution to the given system is $(4,2)$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138090 1069 "e-13130" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138090 1071 "![](images/09/0915c99015922e27855d92ebb13756da634a8f10dd8fe3abbc016590f06e1077.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the number of liters of water in pool $A$ and pool $B$ as a function of time, measured in days. How many days will it take for both pools to contain the same amount of water?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138090 1072 #uuid "86771ced-afc0-5061-a532-bc2345a52ea8" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138090 1074 "The pools have the same amount of water when the two graphs intersect.\n\n![](images/cf/cf7a9b8bdccbc66086c473813523ed893ee192830965300a8c4a0702ad065fc3.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the point $(5,400)$. So, both pools have the same amount ($400\\,\\textrm{L}$) of water at $t=5$ days." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138091 1069 "e-13469" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138091 1071 "Find the value of $\\dfrac 1 3 + 5\\,\\dfrac 1 3$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138091 1072 #uuid "262b82a4-afc9-5d0e-8bf4-12f96fb42fb0" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138091 1074 "To add two mixed numbers, we add\n\n- whole numbers to whole numbers, and\n- fractions to fractions.\n\nFirst, we notice that the first fraction\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac13\\right)\n$$\n\nhas a whole number part of zero.\n\nWith that in mind, let's add our numbers.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$0 + 5 = {\\color{red}5}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$\\dfrac 1 3 + \\dfrac 1 3 = \\dfrac{1+1}{3} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 2 3}}$.\n\nFinally, we combine our results:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 3 + 5\\,\\dfrac 1 3 = {\\color{red}5} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 2 3}} =5\\,\\dfrac 2 3\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138092 1069 "e-13470" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138092 1071 "Find the value of $2\\,\\dfrac 5 {10} + 4\\,\\dfrac 3 {10}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138092 1072 #uuid "c70c6338-9fcf-5672-a5f5-47ff0c8a2bf4" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138092 1074 "To add two mixed numbers, we add\n\n- whole numbers to whole numbers, and\n- fractions to fractions.\n\nWith that in mind let's add our mixed numbers.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$2 + 4 = {\\color{red}{6}}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$\\dfrac 5 {10} + \\dfrac 3 {10} = \\dfrac{5+3}{10} = \\dfrac 8 {10}$.\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$\\dfrac{8}{10} = \\dfrac{8\\div 2}{10\\div 2} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{4}{5}}}$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 5 {10} + 4\\,\\dfrac 3 {10} = {\\color{red}{6}} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 4 5}} = 6\\,\\dfrac 4 5\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138093 1069 "e-13472" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138093 1071 "What is the value of $2\\,\\dfrac 1 4 + 2\\,\\dfrac 3 4$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138093 1072 #uuid "e9686bbd-0755-518a-8553-bb1945cd82bf" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138093 1074 "To add two mixed numbers, we add\n\n- whole numbers to whole numbers, and\n- fractions to fractions.\n\nWith that in mind let's add our mixed numbers.\n\n- Adding the whole numbers, we get\n$2+2 = {\\color{red}{4}}$.\n- Adding the fractions, we get\n$\\dfrac 1 4 + \\dfrac 3 4 = \\dfrac{1+3}{4} = \\dfrac 4 4 = {\\color{blue}{1}}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 4 + 2\\,\\dfrac 3 4 = {\\color{red}{4}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} = 5\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138094 1069 "e-13493" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138094 1071 "Which mixed number is equivalent to $1.9$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138094 1072 #uuid "9e7f1ade-b3a9-58c4-a720-b819ea5953d3" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138094 1074 "Whenever we write a decimal as a mixed number, our mixed number will have\n\n- a *whole number part* that corresponds to the whole number to the *left* of the decimal point, and\n- a *fractional part* that corresponds to the decimal part to the *right* of the decimal point.\n\nWith that in mind, let's write ${\\color{blue}1}.\\!{\\color{red}9}$ as a mixed number.\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}1}$.\n- The decimal part has $1$ decimal place, so we can write it as a fraction in the following way:\n$0.9 = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{9}{10}}$\n\nTherefore, we can write the given decimal as the following mixed number:\n\n$$\n1.9 = 1\\,\\dfrac{9}{10}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138095 1069 "e-13495" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138095 1071 "What is $2.43$ expressed as a mixed number?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138095 1072 #uuid "93220010-9d0a-50b5-9e38-8985efbeb187" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138095 1074 "Whenever we write a decimal as a mixed number, our mixed number will have\n\n- a *whole number part* that corresponds to the whole number to the left of the decimal point, and\n- a *fractional part* that corresponds to the decimal part to the right of the decimal point.\n\nWith that in mind, let's write ${\\color{blue}2}.\\!{\\color{red}43}$ as a mixed number.\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}2}$.\n- The decimal part has $2$ decimal places, so we can write it as a fraction in the following way:\n$0.43 = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{43}{100}}$\n\nTherefore, we can write the given decimal as the following mixed number:\n\n$$\n2.43 = 2\\,\\dfrac{43}{100}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138096 1069 "e-13498" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138096 1071 "Which mixed number is equivalent to $6.213$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138096 1072 #uuid "1a9c8f83-2b35-58f0-8f9b-af0a5ae2627e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138096 1074 "Whenever we write a decimal as a mixed number, our mixed number will have\n\n- a *whole number part* that corresponds to the whole number to the left of the decimal point, and\n- a *fractional part* that corresponds to the decimal part to the right of the decimal point.\n\nWith that in mind, let's write ${\\color{blue}6}.\\!{\\color{red}213}$ as a mixed number.\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}6}$.\n- The decimal part has $3$ decimal places, so we can write it as a fraction in the following way:\n$0.213 = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{213}{1,000}}$\n\nTherefore, we can write the given decimal as the following mixed number:\n\n$$\n6.213 = 6 \\,\\dfrac{213}{1,000}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138097 1069 "e-13500" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138097 1071 "Which mixed number is equivalent to $51.4$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138097 1072 #uuid "be7e80d2-601c-5320-9aa0-f017331c5607" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138097 1074 "Whenever we write a decimal as a mixed number, our mixed number will have\n\n- a *whole number part* that corresponds to the whole number to the left of the decimal point, and\n- a *fractional part* that corresponds to the decimal part to the right of the decimal point.\n\nWith that in mind, let's write ${\\color{blue}51}.\\!{\\color{red}4}$ as a mixed number.\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}51}$.\n- The decimal part has $1$ decimal place, so we can write it as a fraction in the following way:\n$0.4= \\dfrac{4}{10}$\nNow we reduce the fraction to its simplest form by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$\\dfrac{4}{10} = \\dfrac{4 \\div 2}{10 \\div 2} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac{2}{5}}$\n\nTherefore, we can write the given decimal as the following mixed number:\n\n$$\n51.4 = 51 \\, \\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138098 1069 "e-13531" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138098 1071 "Michael bought thirteen T-shirts for his children. The T-shirts cost $\\[math]17$ each. By rounding the number of T-shirts and the cost of each shirt to the nearest ten, estimate the total amount that Michael paid for the T-shirts." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138098 1072 #uuid "f73313f9-a196-5b65-92cc-9fcf712f681a" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138098 1074 "To estimate the total amount that Michael paid, we need to estimate the product $13 \\times 17$.\n\nWe round both factors to the nearest ten:\n$13\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 17 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 10 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}20$\nSo, we now need to find the value of $10 \\times 20$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}10 & ⟶10 \\\\ 20 & ⟶20\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{1} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} = \\color{red}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n${\\color{red}2}{\\color{blue}00}$\n\nTherefore, Michael paid approximately $\\[math]200$ for the T-shirts." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138099 1069 "e-13532" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138099 1071 "A tools factory made $694$ tools each day for $38$ days. By rounding the number of tools and the number of days to the nearest ten, estimate the total number of tools that the factory produced." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138099 1072 #uuid "bca477fc-bc0f-5d0b-bfdc-c52f0f9fcdff" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138099 1074 "To estimate the total number of tools produced, we need to estimate the product $694 \\times 38$.\n\nWe round both factors to the nearest ten, as required:\n$694\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 38 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 690 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}40$\nHence, we need to find the value of $690 \\times 40$.\n\nFirst, we figure out non-zero parts and blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}690 & ⟶690 \\\\ 40 & ⟶40\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that there are $2$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts. We can do this using an area model:\n\n![](images/fa/faa4a2b25fbbee9445b34bbbc9054e140bd48a6ffc296f71d37afdbaf4a35837.png)\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n69\\times 4 = 240+36 = {\\color{red}{276}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}276}{\\color{blue}00} = 27,600\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tools factory produced approximately $27,600$ tools." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138100 1069 "e-13534" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138100 1071 "By rounding the first factor to the nearest ten and the second factor to the nearest hundred, estimate $23 \\times 785$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138100 1072 #uuid "e3804808-4b62-5900-bda3-e422faab6def" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138100 1074 "We round the first factor to the nearest ten and the second factor to the nearest hundred:\n$23\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 785 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 20 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}800$\nHence, we need to find the value of $20 \\times 800$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}20 & ⟶20 \\\\ 800 & ⟶800\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that there are $3$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{8} = \\color{red}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}16}{\\color{blue}000} = 16,000\n$$\n\nTherefore, $23 \\times 785$ is approximately $16,000$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138101 1069 "e-13535" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138101 1071 "Estimate $3,586 \\times 43$ by rounding the first factor to the nearest thousand and the second factor to the nearest ten." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138101 1072 #uuid "2cf7a621-9dfe-5d99-87f8-4c5306d95101" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138101 1074 "We round the first factor to the nearest thousand and the second factor to the nearest ten:\n$3, 586\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 43 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 4, 000 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}40$\nHence, we need to find the value of $4,000 \\times 40$.\n\nFirst, we figure out the non-zero parts and the blocks of zeros for both numbers:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4, 000 & ⟶4000 \\\\ 40 & ⟶40\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe can see that there are $4$ zeros in total.\n\nNext, we multiply the non-zero parts:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} \\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4} = \\color{red}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we join the above with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}16}{\\color{blue}0000} = 160,000\n$$\n\nSo, the estimate is $160,000$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138102 1069 "e-13537" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138102 1071 "Which decimal number is equivalent to $3 \\,\\dfrac{5}{10}$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138102 1072 #uuid "dcdac95a-a4e7-54b9-a14c-c6baa84bb684" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138102 1074 "Whenever we write a mixed number as a decimal, our decimal will have\n\n- a *whole number part* to the left of the decimal point, corresponding to the whole number, and\n- a *decimal part* to the right of the decimal point, corresponding to the fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's write\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} \\,{\\color{red}\\dfrac{5}{10}}\n$$\n\nas a decimal.\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}3}$.\n- The fractional part can be written as a decimal in the following way:\n$\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{10} = 0.{\\color{red}5}$\n\nTherefore, we can write the given mixed number as a decimal number with $1$ decimal place:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{3}} \\,{\\color{red}{\\dfrac{5}{10}}} = {\\color{blue}{3.}}{\\color{red}{5}}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138103 1069 "e-13538" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138103 1071 "What is $11 \\,\\dfrac{3}{100}$ expressed as a decimal?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138103 1072 #uuid "c2455139-0a5b-5d0d-9f1b-0ea5e2195387" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138103 1074 "Whenever we write a mixed number as a decimal, our decimal will have\n\n- a *whole number part* to the left of the decimal point, corresponding to the whole number, and\n- a *decimal part* to the right of the decimal point, corresponding to the fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's write\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}11} \\,{\\color{red}\\dfrac{3}{100}}\n$$\n\nas a decimal.\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}11}$.\n- The fractional part can be written as a decimal in the following way:\n$\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{100} = 0.{\\color{red}03}$\n\nTherefore, we can write the given mixed number as a decimal number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}11} \\,{\\color{red}\\dfrac{3}{100}} = {\\color{blue}11.}{\\color{red}03}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138104 1069 "e-13539" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138104 1071 "What is $2 \\,\\dfrac{31}{1,000}$ expressed as a decimal?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138104 1072 #uuid "2975b26c-53d9-5305-93a5-198405c7be7d" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138104 1074 "Whenever we write a mixed number as a decimal, our decimal will have\n\n- a *whole number part* to the left of the decimal point, corresponding to the whole number, and\n- a *decimal part* to the right of the decimal point, corresponding to the fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's write\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\,{\\color{red}\\dfrac{31}{1,000}}\n$$\n\nas a decimal.\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}2}$.\n- The fractional part can be written as a decimal in the following way:\n$\\dfrac{\\color{red}31}{1,000} = 0.{\\color{red}031}$\n\nTherefore, we can write the given mixed number as a decimal number with $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\,{\\color{red}\\dfrac{31}{1,000}} = {\\color{blue}2.}{\\color{red}{031}}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138105 1069 "e-13542" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138105 1071 "Which decimal number is equivalent to $53 \\,\\dfrac{3}{50}$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138105 1072 #uuid "a0db866a-26df-5c8f-8878-1015478738db" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138105 1074 "Whenever we write a mixed number as a decimal, our decimal will have\n\n- a *whole number part* to the left of the decimal point, corresponding to the whole number, and\n- a *decimal part* to the right of the decimal point, corresponding to the fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's write\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}53} \\,{\\color{red}\\dfrac{3}{50}}\n$$\n\nas a decimal.\n\n- The whole number part is ${\\color{blue}53}$.\n- For the fractional part $\\dfrac{3}{50}$, we can make a denominator of $100$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2{:}$\n$\\dfrac{3}{50} = \\dfrac{3\\times 2}{50 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{6}{100}$\nSo, the fractional part can be represented as a decimal with $2$ decimal places:\n$\\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{100} =0.{\\color{red}06}$\n\nTherefore, we can express our mixed number as a decimal as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}53} \\,{\\color{red}\\dfrac{3}{50}} = {\\color{blue}{53.}}{\\color{red}{06}}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138106 1069 "e-13550" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138106 1071 "What mixed number is equivalent to $\\dfrac{71}{8}$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138106 1072 #uuid "105a0a49-ea48-51f8-961d-269ec910b94c" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138106 1074 "First, we divide $71$ by ${\\color{blue}{8}}{:}$\n\n$$\n71 \\div 8 = 8\\, \\textrm{R}\\,{\\color{red}{7}}\n$$\n\nSo, our division gives $8$ wholes, and we're left with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{71}{8} = 8\\,\\dfrac{\\color{red}7}{\\color{blue}8}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138107 1069 "e-13551" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138107 1071 "Find a mixed number equivalent to $\\dfrac{51}{6}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138107 1072 #uuid "3ba209c9-cae2-5c20-86f4-44c571c223f2" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138107 1074 "First, we divide $51$ by ${\\color{blue}{6}}{:}$\n\n$$\n51 \\div 6 = 8 \\, \\textrm{R}\\, {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo, our division gives $8$ wholes, and we're left with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nWe can reduce the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nto its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{6} &= \\frac{3 \\div 3}{6 \\div 3} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our mixed number is equivalent to\n\n$$\n8 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138108 1069 "e-13554" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138108 1071 "Which decimal number is equivalent to $\\dfrac{8}{10}$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138108 1072 #uuid "91e9792a-ed62-5d4d-b683-0156417dfef2" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138108 1074 "Since we have a denominator of $10$, we can write this fraction as a decimal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{8}{10} &= 0.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138109 1069 "e-13556" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138109 1071 "What is $\\dfrac{25}{100}$ written as a decimal?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138109 1072 #uuid "5ddc2d78-3196-5025-bb01-bda7a21453f3" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138109 1074 "Since we have a denominator of $100$, we can write this fraction as a decimal with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}25}{100} = 0.{\\color{blue}25}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138110 1069 "e-13558" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138110 1071 "Find the decimal number equivalent to $\\dfrac{32}{1,000}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138110 1072 #uuid "6474645d-a8d4-592d-88f9-534b7d328176" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138110 1074 "Since we have a denominator of $1,000$, we can express this fraction as a decimal with $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{32}}{1,000} = 0.0{\\color{blue}{32}}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138111 1069 "e-13567" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138111 1071 "Which of the following is the expanded form of a four-digit number?\n\n1. $3,010 + 100 + 8$\n2. $3,000 + 110 + 8$\n3. $3,000 + 100 + 10 + 8$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138111 1072 #uuid "e12bccd3-75fb-51a0-9fda-77b7cb37cc45" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138111 1074 "A four-digit whole number is in expanded form when it is written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{thousands} +\\textrm{hundreds} + \\textrm{tens} + \\textrm{ones}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our numbers in turn.\n\n- The number $3,010 + 100 + 8$ is *not* written in expanded form because the first term ($3,010$) is a mix of \"thousands\" and \"tens.\"\n- The number $3,000 + 110 + 8$ is *not* written in expanded form because the second term ($110$) is a mix of \"hundreds\" and \"tens.\"\n\n- The number $3,000 + 100 + 10 + 8$ is written in expanded form because\n${\\color{purple}{3}}000+{\\color{blue}{1}}00+{\\color{red}{1}}0+{\\color{magenta}{8}} = {\\color{purple}{3}}\\:\\textrm{thousands} + {\\color{blue}{1}}\\:\\textrm{hundred} + {\\color{red}{1}}\\:\\textrm{ten} + {\\color{magenta}{8}}\\:\\textrm{ones}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138112 1069 "e-13569" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138112 1071 "Which of the following is the expanded form of a three-digit number?\n\n1. $100+1$\n2. $110+1$\n3. $100+10$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138112 1072 #uuid "4c3263e2-1a97-502e-a9f3-f96c68fa333e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138112 1074 "A three-digit whole number is in expanded form when it is written as a sum of exactly three terms:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{hundreds} + \\textrm{tens} + \\textrm{ones}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our numbers in turn.\n\n- The number $100+1$ is written in expanded form because\n${\\color{blue}{1}}00+{\\color{red}{0}}0+{\\color{magenta}{1}} = {\\color{blue}{1}}\\:\\textrm{hundred} + {\\color{red}{0}}\\:\\textrm{tens} + {\\color{magenta}{1}}\\:\\textrm{one}$.\n- The number $110+1$ is *not* written in expanded form because the first term ($110$) is a mix of \"hundreds\" and \"tens.\"\n- The number $100+10$ is written in expanded form because\n${\\color{blue}{1}}00+{\\color{red}{1}}0+{\\color{magenta}{0}} = {\\color{blue}{1}}\\:\\textrm{hundred} + {\\color{red}{1}}\\:\\textrm{ten} + {\\color{magenta}{0}}\\:\\textrm{ones}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138113 1069 "e-13572" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138113 1071 "Which number is equivalent to the expanded form below?\n\n$50,000 + 400 + 6$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138113 1072 #uuid "6e8cd82e-0317-5f65-aa0f-0668485ecf11" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138113 1074 "We need to convert the given number from expanded form to standard form. Notice that:\n\n- $50,000$ is $5$ ten-thousands\n- $400$ is $4$ hundreds\n- $6$ is $6$ ones\n\nWatch out! There are no thousands or tens, which means that we have\n\n- $0$ thousands, and\n- $0$ tens.\n\nPutting this into the place value chart, we get the following:\n\n| **Ten-Thousands** | **Thousands** | **Hundreds** | **Tens** | **Ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $0$ | $6$ |\n\nTherefore, the resulting number is $50,406$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138114 1069 "e-13576" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138114 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing digits in the following equality?\n\n$0$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138114 1072 #uuid "7390ce6b-554d-57b9-8953-950cd6a55e02" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138114 1074 "We need to convert the given number from expanded form to standard form. Notice that:\n\n- $500,000$ is $5$ hundred-thousands\n- $30,000$ is $3$ ten-thousands\n- $6,000$ is $6$ thousands\n- $400$ is $4$ hundreds\n- $20$ is $2$ tens\n- $6$ is $6$ ones\n\nPutting this into a place value chart, we get the following:\n\n| **Hundred-Thousands** | **Ten-Thousands** | **Thousands** | **Hundreds** | **Tens** | **Ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $\\color{blue}6$ | $4$ | $\\color{blue}2$ | $6$ |\n\nTherefore, the resulting number is\n\n$$\n6\n$$\n\nand the missing digits are $6$ and $2$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138115 1069 "e-13582" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138115 1071 "Express $4,385$ in expanded form." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138115 1072 #uuid "86ca35ed-ebf2-5ecd-9b0b-574f7c100fba" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138115 1074 "We need to convert the given number from standard form to expanded form.\n\nLet's start by writing down the place values.\n\n| **Thousands** | **Hundreds** | **Tens** | **Ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $8$ | $5$ |\n\nFrom the table:\n\n- $4$ thousands is $4,000$\n- $3$ hundreds is $300$\n- $8$ tens is $80$\n- $5$ ones is $5$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4,385 = 4,000 + 300 + 80 + 5\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138116 1069 "e-13584" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138116 1071 "Write $900,010$ in expanded form." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138116 1072 #uuid "63a9d04b-14c8-5c0f-87ff-c31d6ed69f06" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138116 1074 "We need to convert the given number from standard form to expanded form.\n\nLet's start by writing down the place values.\n\n| **Hundred-Thousands** | **Ten-Thousands** | **Thousands** | **Hundreds** | **Tens** | **Ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $9$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nFrom the table:\n\n- $9$ hundred-thousands is $900,000$\n- $0$ ten-thousands is $0$\n- $0$ thousands is $0$\n- $0$ hundreds is $0$\n- $1$ ten is $10$\n- $0$ ones is $0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n900, 010 &=  \\\\\n900, 000 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 10 + 0 &=  \\\\\n&= 900, 000 + 10 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138117 1069 "e-13585" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138117 1071 "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$150,210 = 100,000 + 50,000 + \\underline{\\phantom{{}^{55555555555}}} + 10$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138117 1072 #uuid "0bbca3db-469c-52f4-b347-cd5cefe2dcc5" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138117 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values.\n\n| **Hundred-Thousands** | **Ten-Thousands** | **Thousands** | **Hundreds** | **Tens** | **Ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $0$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nFrom the table:\n\n- $1$ hundred-thousand is $100,000$\n- $5$ ten-thousands is $50,000$\n- $0$ thousands is $0$\n- $2$ hundreds is $\\color{blue}200$\n- $1$ ten is $10$\n- $0$ ones is $0$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n150,210 = 100,000 + 50,000 + \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}200} + 10\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is $200$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138118 1069 "e-13586" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138118 1071 "What is $69 \\times 10$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138118 1072 #uuid "53daac7b-c36c-5dce-9305-91317f5580a9" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138118 1074 "To multiply $\\color{red}69$ by $1{\\color{blue}{0}}$, we join together $\\color{red}69$ and $\\color{blue}0{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{69}} \\times 1{\\color{blue}{0}} ={\\color{red}{69}}{\\color{blue}{0}}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138119 1069 "e-13587" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138119 1071 "Find the value of $306 \\times 100$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138119 1072 #uuid "9bb34e43-7301-55d3-ba6b-2f3ad3f1e18a" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138119 1074 "To find ${\\color{red}{306}} \\times 1{\\color{blue}{00}}$, we join together $\\color{red}{306}$ and ${\\color{blue}00}{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{306}}\\times 1{\\color{blue}{00}} = {\\color{red}{306}}{\\color{blue}{00}} = 30,600\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138120 1069 "e-13589" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138120 1071 "Find the value of $190 \\times 1,000$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138120 1072 #uuid "d7314d5e-b8c2-50d1-9ce9-944ff6ace2bd" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138120 1074 "First, notice that $1,000$ has a block of $3$ zeros:\n$3$\n\nNow, to find ${\\color{red}190} \\times 1,000$ we join ${\\color{red}190}$ with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}190}{\\color{blue}000} = 190,000\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n190 \\times 1,000 = 190,000\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138121 1069 "e-13591" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138121 1071 "Find the value of $830 \\times 100,000$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138121 1072 #uuid "de4984eb-1720-5d84-98af-741f624cb216" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138121 1074 "First, notice that $100,000$ has a block of $5$ zeros:\n$5$\n\nNow, to find ${\\color{red}830} \\times 100,000$, we join ${\\color{red}830}$ with the block of $\\color{blue}5$ zeros:\n$5$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n830 \\times 100,000 = 83,000,000\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138122 1069 "e-13619" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138122 1071 "Express $0.41$ as a decimal fraction." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138122 1072 #uuid "85e1ee02-52b9-5f76-8562-58d648acee07" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138122 1074 "Notice that $0.41$ has $2$ decimal places. So, we can write it as a fraction with $100$ in the denominator as follows:\n\n$$\n0.41 = \\dfrac{41}{100}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138123 1069 "e-13621" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138123 1071 "Express $0.048$ as a decimal fraction." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138123 1072 #uuid "554670c4-6a58-5ab6-aafb-5f0647a45060" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138123 1074 "Notice that $0.048$ has $3$ decimal places. So, we can write it as a fraction with $1,000$ in the denominator as follows:\n\n$$\n0.048 = \\dfrac{48}{1,000}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138124 1069 "e-13622" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138124 1071 "Which fraction is equivalent to $0.05$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138124 1072 #uuid "a5996998-a210-5da2-8b86-b3bf579d134a" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138124 1074 "Notice that $0.05$ has $2$ decimal places. So, we can write it as a fraction with $100$ in the denominator:\n\n$$\n0.05 = \\dfrac{5}{100}\n$$\n\nWe then reduce this fraction to its simplest form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5}{100} &= \\frac{5 \\div 5}{100 \\div 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{100 \\div 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10 \\cdot 10 \\div 5} \\\\\n&= (1)/(10 \\cdot (10 \\div 5)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10 \\cdot 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.05$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{20}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138125 1069 "e-13635" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138125 1071 "Find the value of $\\dfrac 2 9 + \\dfrac 3 9$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138125 1072 #uuid "c071fb69-4515-5d7e-94f7-b064584ee53e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138125 1074 "To add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{9} + \\frac{3}{9} = \\frac{2 + 3}{9} = \\frac{5}{9}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138126 1069 "e-13636" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138126 1071 "What is the value of $\\dfrac 3 8 + \\dfrac 1 8$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138126 1072 #uuid "7d309af0-bf42-570a-bc5a-9dc01ca974d5" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138126 1074 "To add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{8} + \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{3 + 1}{8} = \\frac{4}{8}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and the denominator by $4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{8} = \\dfrac{4\\div 4}{8\\div 4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 8 + \\dfrac 1 8 = \\dfrac 1 2\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138127 1069 "e-13637" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138127 1071 "Find the value of $\\dfrac 2 3 + \\dfrac 2 3$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138127 1072 #uuid "f2d34ef1-81be-5999-9df2-9165bef2c53c" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138127 1074 "To add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{2 + 2}{3} = \\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nWe then convert\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 4 3\n$$\n\nto a mixed number:\n\n$$\n4\\div 3 = 1\\,\\textrm{R}1 = 1\\,\\dfrac 1 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 3 + \\dfrac 2 3 = 1\\,\\dfrac 1 3\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138128 1069 "e-13679" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138128 1071 "What is the value of $5\\,\\dfrac{1}{2} + 2\\,\\dfrac 1 6$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138128 1072 #uuid "44ca0914-a69b-5081-bc5a-7d8dd2af01b3" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138128 1074 "To add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions.\n\n**Step 1**: Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n5+2 = {\\color{red}{7}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac 1 6\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we must express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nHere, we can make a common denominator of $6$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $6$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1\\times 3}{2\\times 3} = \\dfrac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{6} + \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{4}{6}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can simplify the above fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6} = \\dfrac{4\\div 2}{6\\div 2} = \\color{blue} \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Combining our results gives:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{7}} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 2 3}} = 7\\,\\dfrac 2 3\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138129 1069 "e-13680" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138129 1071 "Calculate the value of $2\\,\\dfrac{1}{6} + 5\\,\\dfrac 1 8$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138129 1072 #uuid "9626c6b6-39b1-5397-939c-25be582e21b3" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138129 1074 "To add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions.\n\n**Step 1**: Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n2+5 = {\\color{red}{7}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{6} + \\dfrac 1 8\n$$\n\nTo add the fractions, we need to find the least common multiple of the denominators ($6$ and $8$).\n\n- Multiples of $6{:}$ $\\quad 6,12,18,{\\color{purple}{24}},\\ldots$\n- Multiples of $8{:}$ $\\quad 8,16,{\\color{purple}{24}},\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, the least common denominator of $6$ and $8$ is ${\\color{purple}{24}}$.\n\n- To put $\\dfrac 1 6$ over a denominator of $24$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n$\\dfrac{1}{6} = \\dfrac{1\\times 4}{6 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{4}{24}$\n- To put $\\dfrac 1 8$ over a denominator of $24$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n$\\dfrac{1}{8} = \\dfrac{1\\times 3}{8 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{3}{24}$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{4}{24} + \\frac{3}{24} = \\frac{7}{24}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We combine the results to get our final answer:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{7}} + {\\color{blue} \\dfrac{7}{24}} = 7\\,\\dfrac {7}{24}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138130 1069 "e-13682" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138130 1071 "Find the value of $1\\,\\dfrac{1}{2} + 3\\,\\dfrac 3 4$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138130 1072 #uuid "0bf56bec-fdab-5094-8d26-0b57e7522eb8" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138130 1074 "To add two mixed numbers, we add whole numbers to whole numbers and fractions to fractions.\n\n**Step 1**: Adding the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n1+3 = {\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now we add the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac 3 4\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with unlike denominators, we must express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $4$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $4$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1\\times 2}{2\\times 2} = \\dfrac{2}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{4} + \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is an improper fraction. We can convert the fraction\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number in the usual way:\n\n$$\n5\\div 4 = 1\\,\\textrm{R}1 = {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 1{4}}}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We combine the results to get our answer.\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{4}} + {\\color{blue}{1\\,\\dfrac 1{4}}} = 5\\,\\dfrac 1{4}\n$$\n\nNotice that we needed to add the whole numbers one final time." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138131 1069 "e-13686" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138131 1071 "Find the value of $\\dfrac{2}{3} - \\dfrac 1 6$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138131 1072 #uuid "f39c2dd0-f416-5bc4-b282-a732408716da" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138131 1074 "To subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we must express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nSince $\\color{blue}6$ is a multiple of ${\\color{red}{3}}$, we can make a common denominator of ${\\color{blue}{6}}$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{6}}$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{\\color{red}3} = \\dfrac{2\\times 2}{{\\color{red}{3}}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}6}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{6} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\div 3}{6\\div 3} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138132 1069 "e-13687" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138132 1071 "Find the value of $\\dfrac{3}{4} - \\dfrac 1 5$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138132 1072 #uuid "3fa0edc8-0d12-5bb3-bb3a-4550975358b2" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138132 1074 "To subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we must express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nLet's look at the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{\\color{red}4} - \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nThe number ${\\color{red}4}$ is not a multiple of ${\\color{blue}5}$ and vice-versa. But we can make a common denominator of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}4} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 20\n$$\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 {\\color{red}4}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $20$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}5}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{{\\color{red}4}} = \\dfrac{3\\times {\\color{blue}5}}{{\\color{red}4} \\times {\\color{blue}5}} = \\dfrac{15}{20}\n$$\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $20$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{red}4}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{{\\color{blue}5}} = \\dfrac{1\\times {\\color{red}4}}{{\\color{blue}5} \\times {\\color{red}4}} = \\dfrac{4}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{20} - \\frac{4}{20} = \\frac{11}{20}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138133 1069 "e-13688" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138133 1071 "What is the value of $\\dfrac{7}{15} - \\dfrac 2 9$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138133 1072 #uuid "eb6255e6-60a1-54fe-9a04-19cb4108d8e4" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138133 1074 "To subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we must express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nLet's look at the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{15} - \\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nThe denominators $15$ and $9$ share a common factor of $3$. So let's look at their multiples:\n\n- Multiples of $15: \\quad 15,30,{\\color{blue}{45}},60,\\ldots$\n- Multiples of $9: \\quad 9,18,27,36,{\\color{blue}{45}},\\ldots$\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 {15}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $45$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{{15}} = \\dfrac{7\\times 3}{{15} \\times 3} = \\dfrac{21}{45}\n$$\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 9\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $45$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{{9}} = \\dfrac{2\\times 5}{9 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{10}{45}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{45} - \\frac{10}{45} = \\frac{11}{45}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138134 1069 "e-13689" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138134 1071 "What is $40.7 \\times 10$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138134 1072 #uuid "3224b36f-3d52-59fa-851c-79a7bf87d88c" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138134 1074 "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many trailing zeros as we want to the decimal part.\n\n![](images/6e/6e6f8f6b9a5c5f96554897d2a0c1e2eaca75efb8c23775bdde040fbc0b1c30da.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $10$. Since $10$ contains $1$ zero, we move the decimal point $1$ place to the right:\n\n![](images/c1/c192d244c3c0dd74ea292a684688f8bb406aa4729241f9f31f5ec7a5262a6ad7.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/ca/ca9111ca4a3a465ff393687ade7945fa2a14b9b23554e68c2bbb48e1bb35af53.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n\n$$\n407.0 = 407\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n40.7 \\times 10 =407\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138135 1069 "e-13694" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138135 1071 "Find the value of $69.184 \\times 1,000$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138135 1072 #uuid "bbf4f1a9-9ebd-5816-b419-36ac402748dd" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138135 1074 "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many trailing zeros as we want to the decimal part.\n\n![](images/d9/d94ec992f15492a6979cf5b91d281e79a50b0be69188f28b20e88abc253d4260.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $1,000$. Since $1,000$ contains $3$ zeros, we move the decimal point $3$ places to the right:\n\n![](images/d3/d3cef29c48fe7ba498f9455cb6e1050e05190995eb9da91fc24b182e2930584d.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/07/07cf76b10a31970de0d033a1fa556592ce7e8280f55925ba0d1ae54b8058e9fe.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n\n$$\n69,184.0 =69,184\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n69.184 \\times 1,000 = 69,184\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138136 1069 "e-13702" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138136 1071 "What is the value of $65.61 \\times 1,000$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138136 1072 #uuid "5b15b047-8e8b-5598-857d-101e0fef70e4" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138136 1074 "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many trailing zeros as we want to the decimal part.\n\n![](images/8a/8a8218ef71de3eee4477e70f61ca450db44518348c56c83078f9a7b39aa35027.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $1,000$. Since $1,000$ contains $3$ zeros, we move the decimal point $3$ places to the right:\n\n![](images/52/524bcabec3468febde36d918a7435d312b6423e9150d7bc9c0c56f598ca2c8a8.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/b2/b2432641d364363a3174246d8036e1847edd74a429eddffea0667138d5b30071.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n\n$$\n65,610.0 = 65,610\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n65.61 \\times 1,000 = 65,610\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138137 1069 "e-13710" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138137 1071 "Find the value of $70.04 \\times 100,000$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138137 1072 #uuid "2db010c5-1292-51bc-ad88-89dd19fe04a1" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138137 1074 "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many trailing zeros as we want to the decimal part.\n\n![](images/51/51b49a369754e8c97df71fcc674abe9ff4dca0adaa24d6a66b7a2becb0446bca.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $100,000$. Since $100,000$ contains $5$ zeros, we move the decimal point $5$ places to the right:\n\n![](images/c1/c1984ce00f1b104d928eee0c96a2ee94d9ffe04d3da199c82650e2b85b241616.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/a0/a06fa8ff9061355e9fb680c4b981a205ba1577a413370fb2d94c85c115d0721c.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n\n$$\n7,004,000.0 = 7,004,000\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n70.04 \\times 100,000 = 7,004,000\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138138 1069 "e-13738" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138138 1071 "What is the value of $\\dfrac{4}{\\color{blue}15} + \\dfrac 1{\\color{red}5}$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138138 1072 #uuid "6b6c9042-15ac-5a0e-8398-68e7b712c245" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138138 1074 "To add two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nNotice that $\\color{blue}{15}$ is a multiple of ${\\color{red}{5}}$. The multiples of $5$ are:\n\n$$\n5, 10, {\\color{blue}{15}}, 20, \\ldots\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can make a common denominator of ${\\color{blue}{15}}$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {\\color{red}{5}}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of ${\\color{blue}{15}}$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{red}5} = \\dfrac{1\\times 3}{{\\color{red}5}\\times 3} = \\dfrac{3}{\\color{blue}{15}}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{15} + \\frac{3}{15} = \\frac{7}{15}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138139 1069 "e-13739" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138139 1071 "Find the value of $\\dfrac{1}{\\color{red}3} + \\dfrac 1{\\color{blue}5}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138139 1072 #uuid "428ab0f7-87e6-5ca7-8808-e0c5a7763c9f" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138139 1074 "Notice that ${\\color{red}{3}}$ is not a multiple of ${\\color{blue}{5}}$ and vice-versa. But we can make a common denominator of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}{5}} = 15\n$$\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $15$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{blue}{5}}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{red}{3}} = \\dfrac{1\\times {\\color{blue}{5}}}{{\\color{red}{3}} \\times {\\color{blue}{5}}} = \\dfrac{5}{15}\n$$\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {\\color{blue}{5}}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $15$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by ${\\color{red}3}$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{{\\color{blue}{5}}} = \\dfrac{1\\times \\color{red}{3}}{{\\color{blue}{5}} \\times \\color{red}{3}} = \\dfrac{3}{15}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{15} + \\frac{3}{15} = \\frac{8}{15}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138140 1069 "e-13740" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138140 1071 "What is the sum of $\\dfrac{2}{9}$ and $\\dfrac{4}{15}$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138140 1072 #uuid "a61790a4-96d3-5346-b83e-8f70858ee3b9" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138140 1074 "Notice that the denominators ${9}$ and ${15}$ have a common factor of $3$. So let's look at their multiples:\n\n- Multiples of $9: \\quad\\,\\,\\,\\, 9,18,27,36,{\\color{blue}{45}},\\ldots$\n- Multiples of $15: \\quad 15,30,{\\color{blue}{45}},60,\\ldots$\n\nThe least common denominator is ${\\color{blue}{45}}$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $45$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9} = \\dfrac{2\\times 5}{9 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{10}{45}\n$$\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{15}\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $45$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{15} = \\dfrac{4\\times 3}{15 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{12}{45}\n$$\n\nWe can now add the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we add the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{45} + \\frac{12}{45} = \\frac{22}{45}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138141 1069 "e-13752" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138141 1071 "Find the value of $4$ raised to the second power." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138141 1072 #uuid "b5313725-78fc-57ee-9acc-19d03f6624bf" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138141 1074 "We need to evaluate $4^2$.\n\nTo evaluate $4^2$, we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the product:\n\n$$\n4^{2} = 4 \\cdot 4_(⏟)_([math]2[/math] times) = 16\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138142 1069 "e-13754" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138142 1071 "What is the value of $5$ raised to the power of $3$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138142 1072 #uuid "213a088d-3427-5313-adc2-1fcc9d64bb74" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138142 1074 "Here, $5$ raised to the power of $3$ means $5^3$.\n\nTo compute this power, we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n$3$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since\n\n$$\n5 \\times 5 = 25\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 \\cdot 5_(⏟)_(25) \\cdot 5 &= 25 \\cdot 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out ${\\color{blue}25} \\times 5$.\n$_(2); 2\\begin{vmatrix}5 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}5; 1 \\mid 2 \\mid 5$\n\nTherefore, $5^3=125$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138143 1069 "e-13757" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138143 1071 "Find the value of $6^1$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138143 1072 #uuid "0c0e05b6-e633-5b9e-a070-3e26e0349355" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138143 1074 "Here, ${\\color{blue}6}^{\\color{red}1}$ is in exponent form. The base is $\\color{blue}6$ and the exponent is ${\\color{red}1}$.\n\nThis means that $\\color{blue}6$ is taken as a factor only once. Therefore,\n$6^1 = 6$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138144 1069 "e-13760" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138144 1071 "Calculate the value of $0^5$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138144 1072 #uuid "d4e63087-a01f-59b3-bf6c-cf7cbfa3ce78" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138144 1074 "Here, ${\\color{blue}0}^{\\color{red}5}$ is in exponent form. The base is $\\color{blue}0$ and the exponent is ${\\color{red}5}$.\n\nThis means that ${\\color{blue}0}$ is taken as a factor ${\\color{red}5}$ times. Therefore,\n$5$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138145 1069 "e-13767" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138145 1071 "Evaluate $6\\,\\dfrac{1}{2} - 3\\,\\dfrac 3 {10}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138145 1072 #uuid "8f412bc3-e3fb-56da-8ded-e201ca5086c5" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138145 1074 "To subtract two mixed numbers, we subtract whole numbers from whole numbers and fractions from fractions.\n\n**Step 1**: Subtracting the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n6-3 = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now we subtract the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} - \\dfrac 3 {10}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this example, we can make a common denominator of $10$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $10$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1\\times 5}{2\\times 5} = \\dfrac{5}{10}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators.\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{10} - \\frac{3}{10} = \\frac{2}{10}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fraction to its lowest terms.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{10} = \\dfrac{2\\div 2}{10\\div 2} = \\color{blue} \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We combine the results to get our final answer:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}3} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 1 5}} = {\\color{red}{3}}\\,{\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 1 5}}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138146 1069 "e-13769" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138146 1071 "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$0$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138146 1072 #uuid "6227ddee-c908-57ca-aaaf-c39fe162b4db" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138146 1074 "To subtract two mixed numbers, we subtract whole numbers from whole numbers and fractions from fractions.\n\n**Step 1**: Subtracting the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n2-2 = {\\color{red}{0}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now we subtract the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} - \\dfrac 1 4\n$$\n\nLet's look at the denominators. The least common denominator of $3$ and $4$ is $12$.\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 2 3\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $12$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} = \\dfrac{2\\times 4}{3 \\times 4} = \\dfrac{8}{12}\n$$\n\nTo put\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 4\n$$\n\nover a denominator of $12$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{1\\times 3}{4 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{3}{12}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3} - \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{8}{12} - \\frac{3}{12} = \\frac{5}{12}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We now combine the results:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}0} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{5}{12}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{5}{12}}}\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $5$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138147 1069 "e-13770" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138147 1071 "Evaluate $5\\,\\dfrac{3}{4} - 3\\,\\dfrac 1 6$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138147 1072 #uuid "0f46d710-abf6-5fb0-a305-9b2a023555d9" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138147 1074 "To subtract two mixed numbers, we subtract whole numbers from whole numbers and fractions from fractions.\n\n**Step 1**: Subtracting the whole numbers, we get\n\n$$\n5-3 = {\\color{red}{2}}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now we subtract the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} - \\dfrac 1 6\n$$\n\nTo subtract the fractions, we need to find the least common multiple of the denominators ($4$ and $6$).\n\n- Multiples of 4: $\\quad 4,8,{\\color{purple}{12}},\\ldots$\n- Multiples of 6: $\\quad 6,{\\color{purple}{12}},\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, the least common denominator of $4$ and $6$ is ${\\color{purple}{12}}$.\n\n- To put $\\dfrac 3 4$ over a denominator of $12$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n$\\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{3\\times 3}{4 \\times 3} = \\dfrac{9}{12}$\n- To put $\\dfrac 1 6$ over a denominator of $12$, we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n$\\dfrac{1}{6} = \\dfrac{1\\times 2}{6 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{2}{12}$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{9}{12} - \\frac{2}{12} = \\frac{7}{12}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We now combine the results:\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{12}}} = {\\color{red}{2}}\\,{\\color{blue} \\dfrac{7}{12}}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138148 1069 "e-13794" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138148 1071 "By rounding each factor to the nearest whole number, estimate the product $7.7 \\times 5.2$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138148 1072 #uuid "6b1f037f-74ad-5553-8c28-db1c0ac863ef" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138148 1074 "We round both factors to the nearest whole number:\n$7.7\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 5.2 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 8 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}5$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $8 \\times 5$. Computing the product, we get:\n\n$$\n8 \\times 5 = 40\n$$\n\nTherefore, $7.7 \\times 5.2$ is approximately $40$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138149 1069 "e-13795" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138149 1071 "By rounding each factor to the nearest whole number, estimate the product $6.15 \\times 8.64$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138149 1072 #uuid "5e31ed43-49fc-5366-9826-bee7516ce968" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138149 1074 "We round both factors to the nearest whole number:\n$6.15\\begin{vmatrix}\\cdot & 8.64 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 6 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}9$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $6 \\times 9$. Computing the product, we get:\n\n$$\n6 \\times 9 = 54\n$$\n\nTherefore, $6.15 \\times 8.64$ is approximately $54$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138150 1069 "e-13796" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138150 1071 "Luis wants to sell $3.5$ liters of milk at $\\[math] 2.21$ per liter. By rounding the amount of milk to the nearest liter and the price per liter of milk to the nearest dollar, approximate the amount of money that Luis will receive from selling all the milk." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138150 1072 #uuid "1dd9e368-a418-5ee9-b806-18c07c071ec6" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138150 1074 "To find out how much money Luis will receive from selling the milk, we multiply the price of a liter of milk by the amount of milk:\n$2.21 \\times 3.5$\n\nWe want to estimate this product by rounding.\n\n- Rounding the price of a liter of milk to the nearest dollar, we have:\n$2.21 \\quad \\rightarrow\\quad 2$\n- Rounding the amount of milk to the nearest liter, we have:\n$3.5 \\quad \\rightarrow \\quad 4$\n\nSo, we need to find the value of $2 \\times 4$. We get:\n\n$$\n2 \\times 4 = 8\n$$\n\nTherefore, Luis will receive approximately $\\[math] 8$ for the sale of the milk." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138151 1069 "e-13837" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138151 1071 "What is $8.31$ rounded to the nearest whole number?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138151 1072 #uuid "3b0cdacd-1793-5c94-b280-dc50c2a7acbe" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138151 1074 "Rounding a decimal to the nearest whole number means finding the closest whole number to the original decimal.\n\nWe start by locating the digit in the ones place:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{8}}.31\n$$\n\nWe then look one digit to the right and compare it to the number $5$:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{8}}.\\!{\\color{red}{3}}1\n$$\n\nSince $\\color{red}3$ is *less than* $5$, we round down. To do this, we remove all the digits to the right of the decimal point:\n\n$$\n8.31 \\rightarrow 8\n$$\n\nTherefore, $8.31$ rounded to the nearest whole number is $8$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138152 1069 "e-13841" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138152 1071 "What is $76.11$ rounded to the nearest tenth?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138152 1072 #uuid "84beb8f2-7fc3-59f0-8195-1959dce328f3" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138152 1074 "We start by locating the digit in the tenths place:\n\n$$\n76.{\\color{blue}{1}}1\n$$\n\nWe then look one digit to the right and compare it to the number $5$:\n\n$$\n76.{\\color{blue}{1}}{\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nSince $\\color{red}1$ is *less than* $5$, we round down. To do this, we remove all the digits to the right of the tenths place:\n\n$$\n76.11 \\rightarrow 76.1\n$$\n\nTherefore, $76.11$ rounded to the nearest tenth is $76.1$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138153 1069 "e-13844" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138153 1071 "What is $2.343$ rounded to the nearest hundredth?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138153 1072 #uuid "7f079f76-756c-5921-98b2-23d0fea0fd3a" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138153 1074 "We start by locating the digit in the hundredths place:\n\n$$\n2.3{\\color{blue}{4}}3\n$$\n\nWe then look one digit to the right and compare it to the number $5$:\n\n$$\n2.3{\\color{blue}{4}}{\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nSince $\\color{red}3$ is *less than* $5$, we round down. To do this, we remove all the digits to the right of the hundredths place:\n\n$$\n2.343 \\rightarrow 2.34\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2.343$ rounded to the nearest hundredth is $2.34$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138154 1069 "e-13847" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138154 1071 "What is $3.842 \\ldots$ rounded to the nearest tenth?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138154 1072 #uuid "8cfaa5aa-f346-56e1-9d17-b8cf249b35a9" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138154 1074 "We start by locating the digit in the tenths place:\n\n$$\n3.{\\color{blue}{8}}42\\ldots\n$$\n\nWe then look one digit to the right and compare it to the number $5$:\n\n$$\n3.{\\color{blue}{8}}{\\color{red}{4}}2 \\ldots\n$$\n\nSince $\\color{red}4$ is *less than* $5$, we round down. To do this, we remove all the digits to the right of the tenths place:\n\n$$\n3.842 \\ldots \\rightarrow 3.8\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3.842 \\ldots$ rounded to the nearest tenth is $3.8$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138155 1069 "e-13874" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138155 1071 "Find the value of $9.4 \\times 23$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138155 1072 #uuid "e66187e6-2d74-5900-88a2-8c58c7f84902" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138155 1074 "First, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; 9\\begin{vmatrix}.4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 2 \\\\ + & 1 & 8\\end{vmatrix}8\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid 6 \\mid 2\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $9.4$, so the product will also have ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ decimal place.\n\nSo, we take our value of $2162$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $1$ decimal place:\n\n$$\n1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n94 \\times 2.3 = 216.2 \\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138156 1069 "e-13876" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138156 1071 "What is $154$ multiplied by $3.9$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138156 1072 #uuid "d27175bf-0f82-5870-b340-1abb4a01fe0a" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138156 1074 "First, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}4[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}1[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}3[/math] \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 &.9 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 8 & 6 \\\\ + & 4 & 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}0; 6\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $3.9$, so the product will also have ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ decimal place.\n\nSo, we take our value of $6006$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $1$ decimal place:\n\n$$\n1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n154 \\times 3.9 = 600.6 \\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138157 1069 "e-13879" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138157 1071 "What is $7.09$ multiplied by $237$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138157 1072 #uuid "93225702-b473-5499-92e8-2b2724ac0b4c" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138157 1074 "First, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}1[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}2[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}6[/math] \\\\ 7\\end{vmatrix}.0\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}7; 4\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 6 & 3 \\\\ + & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ + & 1\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 8 & 0\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid 3\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $7.09$, so the product will also have ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $168033$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7.09 \\times 237 = 1680.33 \\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138158 1069 "e-13882" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138158 1071 "A roll of adhesive tape is $7.50$ meters long. How many meters of adhesive tape are in $136$ rolls?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138158 1072 #uuid "230545ff-4eef-5447-9e0f-2cbd0c3e6f4a" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138158 1074 "First, notice that $7.50=7.5$.\n\nTo find out how many meters of adhesive tape are in $136$ rolls, we need to multiply $136$ by $7.5$.\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}2[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}1[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}4[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}3[/math] \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 7\\end{vmatrix}.5; 6\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 0 \\\\ + & 9\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}2 \\mid 0 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $7.5$, so the product will also have ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ decimal place.\n\nSo, we take our value of $10200$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place:\n\n$$\n1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n136 \\times 7.5 = 1,020.0 \\,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $136$ rolls of adhesive tape contain $1,020$ meters of tape." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138159 1069 "e-13884" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138159 1071 "What is the value of $6 + 16.4$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138159 1072 #uuid "19136b06-7720-5324-b391-9089ddcb40c6" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138159 1074 "There are no decimal places in $6$ and there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $16.4$.\n\nTo add the numbers, they both need the same number of decimals. Hence, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}1$ decimal zero:\n$1$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n6\\begin{vmatrix}. & 0 \\\\ + & 1\\end{vmatrix}6 \\mid. \\mid 4;\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1; 6\\begin{vmatrix}. & 0 \\\\ + & 1\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}. & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n6 + 16.4 = 22.4\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138160 1069 "e-13888" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138160 1071 "What is $15.4 + 20.79$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138160 1072 #uuid "44ddf017-7985-54a0-95e5-131cebd13a56" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138160 1074 "There is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $15.4$ and there are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $20.79$.\n\nIn order to add the two numbers, we need them to have the same number of decimal places. So, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n15.4=15.4{\\color{red}0}\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}5 &. & 4 & 0 \\\\ + & 2 & 0 &.\\end{vmatrix}7 \\mid 9;\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}5 &. & 4 & 0 \\\\ + & 2 & 0 &.\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}. & 1\\end{vmatrix}9\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n15.4 + 20.79 = 36.19\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138161 1069 "e-13889" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138161 1071 "Calculate $256 \\div 10$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138161 1072 #uuid "135fa171-6d8d-508b-96b9-edca29e2aa54" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138161 1074 "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros to the whole number part as we want:\n\n![](images/cd/cd2429edae47b41137f1049fbd5169a83ebf3463d346d9bd736f74e40defcb15.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $10$. Since $10$ contains $1$ zero, we move the decimal point $1$ place to the left:\n\n![](images/9e/9e067f5edc626c4990f2b1e9b800247e2c4d535d7df0f024218cc9b34a19d1ca.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/c3/c3efba66d65199557ea643ef0ef00a881d806a98901d6dc25f073b3d90c78820.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n\n$$\n25.60 = 25.6\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n256 \\div 10 = 25.6\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138162 1069 "e-13891" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138162 1071 "What is the value of $32.54 + 16.854$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138162 1072 #uuid "1aa57da3-ce99-5d2f-80a7-1742064b5f1a" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138162 1074 "There are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $32.54$ and there are $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places in $16.854$.\n\nTo add the two numbers, we need them to have the same number of decimal places. So, we rewrite the second number so that it has $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n32.54=32.54{\\color{red}0}\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n3\\begin{vmatrix}2 &. & 5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ + & 1 & 6 &. & 8\\end{vmatrix}5 \\mid 4;\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1; 3\\begin{vmatrix}2 &. & 5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ + & 1 & 6 &. & 8\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}9\\begin{vmatrix}. & 3 & 9\\end{vmatrix}4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n32.54 + 16.854 = 49.394\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138163 1069 "e-13894" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138163 1071 "What is the value of $56 + 10.3691$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138163 1072 #uuid "eaddf5a2-efaf-5363-88ed-fa5a2d052d7a" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138163 1074 "There are no decimal places in $56$, and there are $\\color{blue}4$ decimal places in $10.3691$.\n\nTo add the two numbers, we need them to have the same number of decimal places. So, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}4$ decimal places:\n$4$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}6 &. & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ + & 1 & 0 &. & 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix}9 \\mid 1;\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n5\\begin{vmatrix}6 &. & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ + & 1 & 0 &. & 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix}9\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 6\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}. & 3 & 6 & 9\\end{vmatrix}1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n56 + 10.3691 = 66.3691\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138164 1069 "e-13895" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138164 1071 "Calculate $8 \\div 10$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138164 1072 #uuid "3b80fc87-9f27-5653-986c-eb2305eb2b5f" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138164 1074 "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros to the whole number part as we want:\n\n![](images/c9/c9feb4af087a54a16eed8179cc88fc62c3a128eaa2f3d4595e49bbf2056a5958.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $10$. Since $10$ contains $1$ zero, we move the decimal point $1$ place to the left:\n\n![](images/67/6799c901ac93a3d38157e0569589f9b4c9a9087733ea88a8aff69f9249c12a21.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/93/9336332175adc32566e1b8a965c46e2219bc2b8b4da5629d1422702757b7584b.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n$0.80 = 0.8$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n8 \\div 10 = 0.8\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138165 1069 "e-13898" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138165 1071 "Calculate $66.3 \\div 100$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138165 1072 #uuid "2f9cf814-a9ab-5ab3-bd9a-6a6bcfde0b0e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138165 1074 "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros to the whole number part as we want:\n\n![](images/f0/f0c75135166d339375263710870125a922a165c11a487120ad3e7145e5f56773.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $100$. Since $100$ contains $2$ zeros, we move the decimal point $2$ places to the left:\n\n![](images/06/0647f8e29be7464b291e9a93dd167a62bf2e17112dc934d29911fa3bac964c72.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/fa/fa94a68303aa9d99acbea2baac216268487469bc784e3984dda4595bd73224fc.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n$0.663$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n66.3 \\div 100 = 0.663\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138166 1069 "e-13899" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138166 1071 "Find the value of $769 \\div 1,000$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138166 1072 #uuid "8176b084-c620-51ce-89d7-ccde8e9c1dd3" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138166 1074 "First, we write down our number. We can attach as many leading zeros to the whole number part as we want:\n\n![](images/ce/ce1cfcb2ea606dcf5f7d9dd8719c6e3a42ca7a7f02255dc397b403e1046bfbf2.png)\n\nNow, we count the zeros in $1,000$. Since $1,000$ contains $3$ zeros, we move the decimal point $3$ places to the left:\n\n![](images/0c/0c5f8f880698eb9da65a268bb6a301f192d8e6c147502f2628338a0a805c2397.png)\n\nThis gives the following diagram:\n\n![](images/35/354b25663ba585a237e8592a311f1c4f3ff22e9a5af8723627320eeda5f0188a.png)\n\nWe ignore the leading zeros in the whole number part and trailing zeros in the decimal part. This gives the following number:\n\n$$\n0.7690 = 0.769\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n769 \\div 1,000 = 0.769\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138167 1069 "e-14070" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138167 1071 "What is $3.4 \\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ measured in $\\textrm{mm}^2$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138167 1072 #uuid "ea238f6b-7ee7-5cb1-b124-0f07480d5980" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138167 1074 "There are $10\\,\\textrm{mm}$ per $\\textrm{cm}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{cm}}\n$$\n\nWe need to convert this into area units. So, by squaring this quantity and applying the rules of exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{10mm}{cm})^{2} &= \\frac{10^{2}mm^{2}}{cm^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{100mm^{2}}{cm^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $3.4 \\, \\textrm{cm}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3.4cm^{2} &= 3.4cm^{2} \\cdot \\frac{100mm^{2}}{cm^{2}} \\\\\n&= 3.4cm^{2} \\cdot \\frac{100mm^{2}}{cm^{2}} \\\\\n&= 3.4 \\cdot 100mm^{2} \\\\\n&= 340mm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3.4\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ is equivalent to $340\\,\\textrm{mm}^2$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138168 1069 "e-14071" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138168 1071 "What is $0.29\\,\\textrm{m}^2$ measured in $\\textrm{cm}^2$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138168 1072 #uuid "e9ce69f1-ee2f-5f1f-b3fe-e165ddc979df" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138168 1074 "There are $100\\,\\textrm{cm}$ per $\\textrm{m}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{100\\,\\textrm{cm}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nWe need to convert this into area units. So, by squaring this quantity and applying the rules of exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{100cm}{m})^{2} &= \\frac{100^{2}cm^{2}}{m^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10000cm^{2}}{m^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $0.29\\,\\textrm{m}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.29m^{2} &= 0.29m^{2} \\cdot \\frac{10000cm^{2}}{m^{2}} \\\\\n&= 0.29m^{2} \\cdot \\frac{10000cm^{2}}{m^{2}} \\\\\n&= 0.29 \\cdot 10000cm^{2} \\\\\n&= 2900cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138169 1069 "e-14072" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138169 1071 "What is $0.053\\,\\textrm{km}^2$ measured in $\\textrm{m}^2$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138169 1072 #uuid "e99aff48-d63a-559f-b97b-f565fa8894cd" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138169 1074 "There are $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ per $\\textrm{km}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\,000\\,\\textrm m}{\\textrm{km}}\n$$\n\nWe need to convert this into area units. So, by squaring this quantity and applying the rules of exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1000m}{km})^{2} &= \\frac{1000^{2}m^{2}}{km^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1000000m^{2}}{km^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $0.053\\,\\textrm{km}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.053km^{2} &= 0.053km^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1000000m^{2}}{km^{2}} \\\\\n&= 0.053km^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1000000m^{2}}{km^{2}} \\\\\n&= 0.053 \\cdot 1000000m^{2} \\\\\n&= 53000m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.053\\,\\textrm{km}^2$ is equivalent to $53\\,000 \\,\\textrm{m}^2$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138170 1069 "e-14084" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138170 1071 "The height $h$ of a lamppost is $30\\,\\textrm{ft}$ to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138170 1072 #uuid "b18fef84-bbbb-58c6-a215-4146461c28f9" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138170 1074 "Our measurement is given to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$. So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\,\\textrm{ft}}{2} = 0.5\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlower bound &= 30ft - 0.5ft \\\\\n&= 29.5ft \\\\\nupper bound &= 30ft + 0.5ft \\\\\n&= 30.5ft\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $h$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n29.5\\,\\textrm{ft} \\leq h < 30.5\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138171 1069 "e-14085" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138171 1071 "The area $a$ of a golf course measures $0.6\\,\\textrm{km}^2$ to the nearest tenth of a square kilometer. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138171 1072 #uuid "9e0f0485-0635-558e-8493-eef61ee623f7" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138171 1074 "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\textrm{km}^2$. So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.1\\,\\textrm{km}^2}{2} = 0.05\\,\\textrm{km}^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlower bound &= 0.6km^{2} - 0.05km^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.55km^{2} \\\\\nupper bound &= 0.6km^{2} + 0.05km^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.65km^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $a$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.55\\,\\textrm{km}^2 \\leq a < 0.65\\,\\textrm{km}^2\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138172 1069 "e-14086" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138172 1071 "The force $F$, measured in $\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2$, applied to a motorcycle varies directly with its acceleration $a$, measured in $\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}^2$. When the acceleration of the motorcycle is $a=5\\,\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}^2$, the force is $F=2\\,000\\,\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2$. What is the constant of variation?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138172 1072 #uuid "f01f91de-9010-5429-ba83-faaf90c866d7" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138172 1074 "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the acceleration $a$, so we have\n\n$$\nF = k{a}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the acceleration is\n\n$$\na=5\\, \\textrm{m}/\\,\\textrm{s}^2\n$$\n\nthe force is\n\n$$\nF=2\\,000\\,\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2\n$$\n\nWe can use this information to solve for $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2000 &= k \\cdot (5) \\\\\n2000 &= 5k \\\\\n\\frac{2000}{5} &= k \\\\\n400 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{F}{a}\n$$\n\nwe have that the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{kg m/s^{2}}{m/s^{2}} &= \\frac{kgm/s^{2}}{m/s^{2}} \\\\\n&= kg\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\nk = 400\\,\\textrm{kg}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138173 1069 "e-14087" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138173 1071 "The dimensions of a parking lot are $90\\, \\textrm{m} \\times 50\\, \\textrm{m}$, where each dimension is measured to the nearest $10\\, \\textrm{m}$. Rounded to the nearest ten square meters, what is the upper bound for the area of the parking lot?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138173 1072 #uuid "44ff13ac-1980-554e-aea8-4aaa51d202fc" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138173 1074 "Each dimension is given to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{m}$. So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{m}}{2} = 5\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *upper bound* for the area of the parking lot. Therefore, we require the *upper bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe upper bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n90m + 5m = 95m; 50m + 5m = 55m\n$$\n\nSo, the upper bound for the area of the parking lot is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= 95m \\cdot 55m \\\\\n&= 5225 \\\\\n&\\approx 5230m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest ten square meters." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138174 1069 "e-14088" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138174 1071 "The stretch $s$, measured in centimeters $(\\textrm{cm})$, of a loaded spring varies directly with the load $l$, measured in grams $(\\mathrm{g})$. When the load is $l=8\\,\\mathrm{g}$, the stretch is $s=9.6\\,\\mathrm{cm}$. Find the load when the stretch is $s=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138174 1072 #uuid "f1408bad-ae5c-55fb-b78e-60ee0d8a1ca0" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138174 1074 "The stretch $s$ is directly proportional to the load $l$, so we have\n\n$s = k{l}$,\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when\n\n$$\nl=8\\,\\mathrm{g}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\ns=9.6\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nWe can use this information to solve for $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9.6 &= k \\cdot (8) \\\\\n9.6 &= 8k \\\\\n\\frac{9.6}{8} &= k \\\\\n1.2 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\ns = 1.2l\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the load when the stretch is\n\n$$\ns=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nl &= \\frac{s}{1.2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7.2}{1.2} \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the load when\n\n$$\ns=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nl=6 \\,\\textrm{g}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138175 1069 "e-14089" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138175 1071 "In an inverse variation, $y=5$ when $x=8$. Find an equation that relates $x$ and $y$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138175 1072 #uuid "8a12b2fd-6361-59ef-8378-6c900a1ab47b" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138175 1074 "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x$, we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{k}{x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the data $y=5$ when $x=8$ to find $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 &= \\frac{k}{8} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 8 &= k \\\\\n40 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our inverse variation equation is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{40}{x}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138176 1069 "e-14090" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138176 1071 "If $y$ varies inversely with $x$ and $y=15$ when $x=\\dfrac{3}{5}$, find the value of $y$ when $x=3$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138176 1072 #uuid "a2d2a2a1-da7b-5106-8347-3a4f71ddd619" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138176 1074 "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x$, we use the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{k}{x}\n$$\n\nWe are told that $y=15$ when\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this data into the equation to find $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15 &= (k)/((\\frac{3}{5})) \\\\\n15 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{5}) &= k \\\\\n\\frac{45}{5} &= k \\\\\n9 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the inverse variation is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{9}{x}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the value of $x=3$ into this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{9}{3} = 3\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138177 1069 "e-14091" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138177 1071 "The force $F$, in newtons ($\\mathrm N$), that an elastic spring exerts on an object varies directly with the **square** of the extension of the spring $L$, measured in meters. If the force is $F = 4\\,\\mathrm N$ when $L=0.1\\,\\mathrm m$, then what equation models this situation?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138177 1072 #uuid "201cba9a-1d9e-5bf3-b312-d84fb3d59407" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138177 1074 "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the square of the spring extension $L$, so we have\n$F = kL^2$,\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that\n\n$$\nF=4\\,\\textrm{N}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nL=0.1\\,\\textrm m\n$$\n\nWe can use this information to solve for $k$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= k \\cdot (0.1)^{2} \\\\\n4 &= k \\cdot 0.01 \\\\\n\\frac{4}{0.01} &= k \\\\\n400 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n$F = 400L^2$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138178 1069 "e-14092" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138178 1071 "| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $0.5$ | $16$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n\nConsider the table above. Which of the following statements is true?\n\n1. $y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with constant of variation $k=8$.\n2. $y$ is inversely proportional to $x$ with constant of variation $k=16$.\n3. $y$ is not inversely proportional to $x$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138178 1072 #uuid "80333f8b-9aff-5f31-8301-a8708d531a22" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138178 1074 "If $y$ varies inversely with $x$, then we must have\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{k}{x}\n$$\n\nfor some constant $k$. Note that we can rewrite the above equation as follows.\n\n$$\nx \\cdot y = k\n$$\n\nSo, we will compute\n\n$$\nx\\cdot y = k\n$$\n\nfor each row in the table. If we get the same value of $k$ for each row, then we can conclude that $y$ is inversely proportional to $x$.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $x\\cdot y = k$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.5$ | $16$ | $0.5\\cdot 16=8$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $2\\cdot 4=8$ |\n| $4$ | $2$ | $4\\cdot 2=8$ |\n\nThe results are all the same: in each row, we have $k=8$. Therefore, $y$ varies inversely with $x$, and the constant of variation is $k=8$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138179 1069 "e-14094" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138179 1071 "![](images/02/02cf003d5046e2c6486cc11df95d8fae60bf9981ec8f0934621f18b4fa509153.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the relation between $x$ and $y$. Given that $y$ varies inversely with $x$, find the inverse variation equation between $x$ and $y$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138179 1072 #uuid "9c285374-0465-52ed-a283-c469e123d167" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138179 1074 "Since $y$ varies inversely with $x$, we use the equation\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{k}{x}\n$$\n\nIn the graph, we notice a point with $x=5$ and $y=5$. We substitute this data into the equation to find $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 &= \\frac{k}{5} \\\\\n5 \\cdot 5 &= k \\\\\n25 &= k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse variation equation is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{25}{x}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138180 1069 "e-14180" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138180 1071 "What expression is equivalent to $8 \\times 8 \\times 8 \\times 8$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138180 1072 #uuid "ebca2372-6332-546a-865a-b1389c08dd49" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138180 1074 "In this question, $\\color{blue}8$ is multiplied by itself $\\color{red}4$ times:\n$4$\n\nSo, in exponent form, the base must be $\\color{blue}8$ and the exponent must be ${\\color{red}4}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} \\times {\\color{blue}8} \\times {\\color{blue}8} \\times {\\color{blue}8} = {\\color{blue}8}^{\\color{red}4}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138181 1069 "e-14181" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138181 1071 "What is $18 \\times 18 \\times 18$ written as a power?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138181 1072 #uuid "cd28f5fb-117d-5290-b89e-78d59d73cf58" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138181 1074 "In this question, $\\color{blue}18$ is multiplied by itself $\\color{red}3$ times:\n$3$\n\nSo, in exponent form, the base must be $\\color{blue}18$ and the exponent must be ${\\color{red}3}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{\\color{blue}18} \\times {\\color{blue}18} \\times {\\color{blue}18}}= {\\color{blue}18}^{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Notice that ${\\color{blue}18}^{\\color{red}3}$ means that the base is ${\\color{blue}18}$, **not** $8$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138182 1069 "e-14183" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138182 1071 "Write down $6^5$ using multiplication signs." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138182 1072 #uuid "4d103ec6-54ce-5bee-b1f4-9f36f468cddc" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138182 1074 "Here, ${\\color{blue}6}^{\\color{red}5}$ is in exponent form. The base is $\\color{blue}6$ and the exponent is ${\\color{red}5}$.\n\nThis means that $\\color{blue}6$ is multiplied by itself $\\color{red}5$ times:\n$5$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n6^5 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 \\times 6\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138183 1069 "e-14185" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138183 1071 "What is $234^4$ expressed using multiplication signs?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138183 1072 #uuid "10c85ed5-dea4-53d9-8d5a-5588c724de5a" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138183 1074 "We notice that ${\\color{blue}234}^{\\color{red}4}$ is in exponent form.\n\nFirst, note that the base is **NOT** $4$. The base is **NOT** $34$ either.\n\nInstead, the base is the full number ${\\color{blue}234}$, and the exponent is ${\\color{red}4}$. This means that $\\color{blue}234$ is multiplied by itself $\\color{red}4$ times:\n$4$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n234^4 = 234 \\times 234 \\times 234 \\times 234\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138184 1069 "e-14186" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138184 1071 "What is the value of $29 \\times 10^1$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138184 1072 #uuid "a52b00d0-8444-5aee-9453-adc406fcc29d" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138184 1074 "First, we can expand $10^1$ by writing a $1$ followed by a block of $1$ zero:\n\n$$\n1\n$$\n\nNow, to find $29 \\times 10$, we join $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{29}$ with the block of zeros:\n$1$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n29 \\times 10^1 = 290\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138185 1069 "e-14189" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138185 1071 "Tom buys $7$ reams of paper. Each ream consists of $10^2$ sheets of paper. How many sheets of paper did Tom buy in total?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138185 1072 #uuid "18dc9acf-e138-51fe-a603-21217df3f11d" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138185 1074 "To calculate the total number of sheets of paper, we need to multiply $7$ by $10^2$.\n\nFirst, we notice that\n$2$\n\nSo, we have\n$2$\n\nNow, to find $7 \\times 100$, we join $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{7}$ with the block of zeros:\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7 \\times 10^2 = 700\n$$\n\nWe conclude that Tom bought $700$ sheets of paper." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138186 1069 "e-14190" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138186 1071 "Find the value of $173 \\times 10^3$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138186 1072 #uuid "672d7c34-3b8b-5a67-ac8b-084f877874a6" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138186 1074 "First, we can expand out $10^3$ by writing a $1$ followed by a block of $3$ zeros:\n\n$$\n3\n$$\n\nSo, we have:\n\n$$\n3\n$$\n\nNow, to find $173 \\times 1,000$, we join $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{173}$ with the block of zeros:\n$3$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n173 \\times 10^3 = 173,000\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138187 1069 "e-14194" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138187 1071 "$582 \\times 10^4=$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138187 1072 #uuid "63479b62-22cf-547d-8436-cbaf1774bc14" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138187 1074 "First, we can expand out $10^4$ by writing a $1$ followed by a block of $4$ zeros:\n$4$\n\nSo, we have:\n\n$$\n4\n$$\n\nNow, to find $582 \\times 10,000$, we join $\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{582}$ with the block of zeros:\n$4$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n582 \\times 10^4 = 5,820,000\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138188 1069 "e-14195" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138188 1071 "What is $0.27 \\times 0.6$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138188 1072 #uuid "0bcab7e9-ceb8-5471-bcd4-0c4ac0348335" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138188 1074 "First, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers. We ignore any leading ${\\color{red}{0}}$'s during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n4; 0\\begin{vmatrix}.2 & 7 \\\\ \\cdot & 0\\end{vmatrix}.6; 1 \\mid 6 \\mid 2\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $0.27$ and there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $0.6$, so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} = 3\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $162$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.27 \\times 0.6 = 0.162 \\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138189 1069 "e-14198" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138189 1071 "Find the value of $3.46 \\times 0.2$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138189 1072 #uuid "9f20cc27-7dc6-5484-9a20-f39f120a7f79" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138189 1074 "First, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers. We ignore any leading ${\\color{red}{0}}$'s during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n0\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}.4\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}.2 \\\\ 6\\end{vmatrix}9 \\mid 2\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $3.46$ and there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $0.2$, so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} + {\\color{blue}1} = 3\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $692$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3.46 \\times 0.2 = 0.692 \\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138190 1069 "e-14200" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138190 1071 "What is $3.2$ multiplied by $1.06$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138190 1072 #uuid "86834a4d-9d8c-5cc6-861c-9c71ae46043d" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138190 1074 "First, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; 3\\begin{vmatrix}.2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 &.0\\end{vmatrix}6; 1\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ + & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ + & 3\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}9 \\mid 2\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $3.2$ and there are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $1.06$, so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}} + {\\color{blue}{2}} = 3\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $3392$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3.2 \\times 1.06 = 3.392 \\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138191 1069 "e-14202" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138191 1071 "By riding her tricycle, Lisa can cover a distance of $0.03$ miles in a minute. How far can she travel if she cycles for $8.5$ minutes?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138191 1072 #uuid "c6727bb1-1b12-582b-8cef-6bd6ca7aba56" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138191 1074 "To determine how far Lisa can travel in $8.5$ minutes, we have to multiply $8.5$ by $0.03$.\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers. We ignore any leading ${\\color{red}{0}}$'s during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n1; 8\\begin{vmatrix}.5 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}.0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}5\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $8.5$ and there are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $0.03$, so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}} + {\\color{blue}{2}} = 3\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $255$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n8.5 \\times 0.03 = 0.255 \\,\n$$\n\nWe conclude that Lisa could cover a distance of $0.255$ miles in $8.5$ minutes." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138192 1069 "e-14323" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138192 1071 "Expand $(x + 3)(x - 3)$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138192 1072 #uuid "6d8e25fc-b9a0-5cd1-9a33-3e7c6d34d2c9" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138192 1074 "We will use the difference of square formula:\n\n$$\n(a + b)(a - b) = a^2 - b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=x$ and $b=3$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (x + 3)(x - 3) \\\\[5pt] &= x^2 - 3^2 \\\\[5pt] &= x^2 - 9. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138193 1069 "e-14326" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138193 1071 "Expand $(5x - 4)(5x + 4)$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138193 1072 #uuid "092e54fe-57ef-5243-a7a1-5ac10d3a0dca" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138193 1074 "We will use the difference of square formula:\n\n$$\n(a + b)(a - b) = a^2 - b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=5x$ and $b=4$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (5x - 4)(5x + 4) \\\\[5pt] &= (5x)^2 - (4)^2 \\\\[5pt] &= 25x^2 - 16. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138194 1069 "e-14329" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138194 1071 "Expand $(6x + y^2)(6x - y^2)$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138194 1072 #uuid "fc349f72-6389-5a1d-b56f-69afe3af3d41" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138194 1074 "We will use the difference of square formula:\n\n$$\n(a + b)(a - b) = a^2 - b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=6x$ and $b=y^2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (6x + y^2)(6x - y^2) \\\\[5pt] &= (6x)^2 - \\left(y^2\\right)^2 \\\\[5pt] &= 36x^2 - y^4. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138195 1069 "e-14337" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138195 1071 "Expand $(2x^n-1)(2x^n+1)$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138195 1072 #uuid "eeb682a3-2246-55f7-b99c-f60c589a0587" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138195 1074 "We will use the difference of square formula:\n\n$$\n(a + b)(a - b) = a^2 - b^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2x^n$ and $b=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2x^{n} - 1)(2x^{n} + 1) &=  \\\\\n(2x^{n})^{2} - (1)^{2} &=  \\\\\n2^{2} \\cdot (x^{n})^{2} - 1 &=  \\\\\n&= 4x^{2n} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138196 1069 "e-14415" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138196 1071 "What is the value of $5.98-0.432$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138196 1072 #uuid "11f96846-5eff-52b6-8d1d-efa1e948cf1e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138196 1074 "There are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $5.98$ and there are $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places in $0.432$.\n\nTo subtract the numbers, they both need the same number of decimals. Therefore, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n5.98=5.98{\\color{red}0}\n$$\n\nNow, we line up the decimal points:\n$5\\begin{vmatrix}. & 9 & 8 & 0 \\\\ - & 0 &. & 4\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid 2;$.\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n$7\\begin{vmatrix}10 \\\\ 5\\end{vmatrix}.\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 8 & 0 \\\\ - & 0 &.\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 &.\\end{vmatrix}5 \\mid 4 \\mid 8$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n5.98-0.432= 5.548\\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138197 1069 "e-14417" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138197 1071 "Find the value of $9.4-0.254$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138197 1072 #uuid "fa5dabe8-4509-5878-87ef-9840051aee69" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138197 1074 "There are is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $9.4$ and there are $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places in $0.254$.\n\nTo subtract the numbers, they both need the same number of decimals. Therefore, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n9.4 = 9.4{\\color{red}00}\n$$\n\nNow, we line up the decimal points:\n$9\\begin{vmatrix}. & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ - & 0 &. & 2\\end{vmatrix}5 \\mid 4;$.\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n$3\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 10 \\\\ 9 &.\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ - & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 9 &. & 1\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 6$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n9.4-0.254=9.146\\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138198 1069 "e-14419" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138198 1071 "What is $1-0.56$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138198 1072 #uuid "5f6f4cee-7501-5b2e-a554-7efd75f53edc" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138198 1074 "There are no decimal places in $1$ and there are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $0.56$.\n\nTo subtract the numbers, they both need the same number of decimals. Therefore, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places.\n$2$\n\nNow, we line up the decimal points:\n$1\\begin{vmatrix}. & 0 & 0 \\\\ - & 0 &.\\end{vmatrix}5 \\mid 6;$.\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n$0\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 10 \\\\ 1 &.\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}. & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 &. & 4\\end{vmatrix}4$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n1-0.56 = 0.44\\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138199 1069 "e-14421" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138199 1071 "Carol bought $3$ yards of polyester fabric to make two shirts. She cut the fabric into two pieces and found that one piece was $1.4$ yards long. How long was the other piece of fabric?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138199 1072 #uuid "d5475d5f-20c3-53da-8d1f-578cfdbd2510" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138199 1074 "To find the length of the second piece of fabric, we need to calculate the difference $3 - 1.4$.\n\nThere are $\\color{blue}0$ decimal places in $3$ and there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $1.4$.\n\nTo subtract the numbers, both need to have the same number of decimals. Hence, we rewrite the first number so that it has $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place:\n$1$\n\nFirst, we line up the decimal points:\n$3\\begin{vmatrix}. & 0 \\\\ - & 1\\end{vmatrix}. \\mid 4;$.\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n$2\\begin{vmatrix}10 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}.\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}. & 4 \\\\ 1 &.\\end{vmatrix}6$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3 - 1.4 = 1.6\n$$\n\nand we conclude that the second piece was $1.6$ yards long." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138200 1069 "e-14468" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138200 1071 "Use a place value chart to calculate the value of $232 \\times 10$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138200 1072 #uuid "a255042f-baf8-54e9-aae6-a0c1413c255b" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138200 1074 "**Step 1.** We write down the place value chart for $232$:\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\n**Step 2.** Since we're multiplying by $10$, we move all digits to the left by $1$ place and place a zero in the empty cell on the right.\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $3$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n232\\times 10 = 2,320\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138201 1069 "e-14471" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138201 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing digits in the place value chart below for the value of $67 \\times 100$?\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $6$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138201 1072 #uuid "0776770e-af9f-527e-b837-943f5e7a976e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138201 1074 "**Step 1:** We write down the place value chart for $67$:\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n\n**Step 2:** Since $1{\\color{blue}{00}}$ contains a block of $\\color{blue}2$ zeros, we move all values to the left by $2$ places and place zeros in the empty cells on the right.\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $6$ | $7$ | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$ | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$ |\n\nTherefore, the missing digits are $7$ and $0$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138202 1069 "e-14472" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138202 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing digits in the place value chart below for the value of $15 \\times 1,000$?\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138202 1072 #uuid "ca1e1a0d-7b0c-538c-993b-5dc2f4014bdf" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138202 1074 "We proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We write down the place value chart for $15$:\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n\n**Step 2.** Notice that $1,{\\color{blue}000}$ contains a block of $3$ zeros. So, we move all digits to the left by $3$ places and place zeros in the empty cells on the right.\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{5}$ | $0$ | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$ | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$ |\n\nTherefore, the missing digits are $5,\\,0$, and $0$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138203 1069 "e-14486" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138203 1071 "Find the value of $727 \\times 6$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138203 1072 #uuid "356f804e-2ae3-5582-a957-25a07e42fb3f" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138203 1074 "We write it this way:\n\n$$\n7 \\mid 2\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n_(4); 7 \\mid 2\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}6; 2 \\mid 7 \\cdot 6 = 42; Carry:4; Write:2\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\left._(1)\\right|_(4); 7\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ \\cdot & 6 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}2 \\cdot 6 = 12and4 + 12 = 16; Carry:1; Write:6\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the hundreds:\n\n$$\n\\left._(1)\\right|_(4); 7\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ \\cdot & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}6 \\mid 2 \\mid 7 \\cdot 6 = 42and1 + 42 = 43; Carry:-; Write:43\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n727 \\times 6 = 4 \\, 362\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138204 1069 "e-14487" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138204 1071 "A bag of acorn seeds contains $2,162$ seeds in total. Mary purchases $4$ bags. How many seeds does she have in total?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138204 1072 #uuid "cb012a75-fa88-5966-852d-2a82c8753c1b" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138204 1074 "To calculate the total number of seeds, we need to multiply $2,162$ by $4$:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot \\mid 4 \\mid 24and1 + 24 = 250000\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones. Notice that there is no digit to carry.\n\n$$\n_(); 2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 8\\end{vmatrix}2 \\cdot 4 = 80000000000000000; Carry: -; Write:8\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens. This time there is a digit to carry, which we should place above the hundreds column.\n\n$$\n\\left._(2)\\right|_(); 2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid 6 \\cdot 4 = 240000000000000000; Carry:2; Write:4\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the hundreds:\n\n$$\n_(2); 2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 8 \\mid 1 \\cdot 4 = 4and2 + 4 = 6; Carry: -; Write:6\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the thousands:\n\n$$\n_(2); 2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 8\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}2 \\cdot 4 = 80000000000000000; Carry:-; Write:8\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2,162 \\times 4 = 8, 648\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138205 1069 "e-14522" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138205 1071 "Simplify $4.90$ to its simplest form. What is the equivalent decimal number?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138205 1072 #uuid "692c374a-57f2-5ab6-b6e9-14d404c12bfe" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138205 1074 "To find an equivalent decimal, we remove the zero at the end.\n\n$$\n4.9\\!\\cancel{0} = 4.9\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4.90$ and $4.9$ are equivalent decimals." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138206 1069 "e-14524" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138206 1071 "Find a decimal that's equivalent to $89.00$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138206 1072 #uuid "5193fbde-54f7-513b-be86-013257bb54de" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138206 1074 "To find an equivalent decimal, we remove the zeros at the end.\n\n$$\n89.\\!\\cancel{00} = 89\n$$\n\nTherefore, $89.00$ and $89$ are equivalent decimals." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138207 1069 "e-14525" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138207 1071 "What is $28.6$ multiplied by $7$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138207 1072 #uuid "ac74444f-6dcb-5e20-bfa4-13c1b865dd26" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138207 1074 "First, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n6\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}8\\begin{vmatrix}.6 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}7; 2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}2\n$$\n\nThen, we insert the decimal point in the result to have the same number of decimal places as the decimal factor.\n\nThere is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $28.6$, so the product will also have ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ decimal place. We take our value of $2002$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place:\n\n$$\n1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n28.6 \\times 7 = 200.2 \\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138208 1069 "e-14526" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138208 1071 "Find a decimal that's equivalent to $12$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138208 1072 #uuid "57679600-90c8-5b49-9735-e7b68500af76" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138208 1074 "To find an equivalent decimal, we can attach a zero to the end, but only *after* the decimal point!\n\n$$\n12 = 12.{\\color{blue}{0}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12$ and $12.0$ are equivalent." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138209 1069 "e-14527" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138209 1071 "One ton of a certain solution contains $0.06$ grams of gold. How much gold is there in $0.09$ tons of the solution?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138209 1072 #uuid "491bdec4-e6d4-51dd-9105-761b914a2008" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138209 1074 "To find the weight of gold in $0.09$ tons of the solution, we need to calculate $0.06 \\times 0.09$.\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers. We ignore any leading zeros during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n6 \\times 9 = 54\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $0.06$ and there are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $0.09$, so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} + {\\color{blue}2} = 4\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $54$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $4$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.09$ tons of the solution will contain $0.0054$ grams of gold." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138210 1069 "e-14529" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138210 1071 "What is $13.96$ multiplied by $6$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138210 1072 #uuid "42455e66-3d23-5f22-a44a-c1514c275817" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138210 1074 "First, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}.9\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}6; 8\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 7\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\nThen, we insert the decimal point in the result to have the same number of decimal places as the decimal factor.\n\nThere are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $13.96$, so the product will also have ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ decimal places. We take our value of $8376$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n13.96 \\times 6 = 83.76 \\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138211 1069 "e-14530" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138211 1071 "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$0$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138211 1072 #uuid "5bac2102-d6cd-5042-a164-d649d339862a" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138211 1074 "To find an equivalent decimal, we can add extra zeros to the end.\n\n$$\n361 = 361. \\bbox[2px,lightgray]{\\color{blue}0} 0 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing digit is $0$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138212 1069 "e-14531" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138212 1071 "What is $0.05 \\times 0.62$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138212 1072 #uuid "40e0bc00-c8e4-5970-900a-f7091b33e920" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138212 1074 "First, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers. We ignore any leading $\\color{red}0$'s during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n0\\begin{vmatrix}.0 & 5 \\\\ \\cdot & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}.6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ + & 3\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $0.05$ and there are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $0.62$, so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{2}} = 4\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $310$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $4$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.05 \\times 0.62 = 0.0310 = 0.031 \\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138213 1069 "e-14532" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138213 1071 "$0.614 \\times 3 =$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138213 1072 #uuid "a7e91544-1f03-5c0c-a4a2-d5db780fd4e0" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138213 1074 "First, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 &.6\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}3; 1\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4\\end{vmatrix}2\n$$\n\nThen, we insert the decimal point in the result to have the same number of decimal places as the decimal factor.\n\nThere are $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places in $0.614$, so the product will also have ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ decimal places. We take our value of $1842$ and place a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{blue}3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n3\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.614 \\times 3 = 1.842\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138214 1069 "e-14533" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138214 1071 "Find the value of $0.36 \\times 0.47$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138214 1072 #uuid "8287c120-300d-5f0b-aabc-023b2a70ac9c" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138214 1074 "First, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers. We ignore any leading $\\color{red}0$'s during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}2[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}4[/math]; 0\\begin{vmatrix}.3 & 6 \\\\ \\cdot & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}.4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2; +\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}0; 1\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 9\\end{vmatrix}2\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $0.36$ and there are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $0.47$, so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}} + {\\color{blue}{2}} = 4\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $1692$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $4$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.36 \\times 0.47 = 0.1692 \\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138215 1069 "e-14534" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138215 1071 "Bart bought $4$ tickets to go with his friends to the movie theater. If he paid $\\[math]12.69$ for each ticket, how much did Bart spend in total to buy the tickets?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138215 1072 #uuid "dccb3bef-5635-56e7-a35a-b29223591703" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138215 1074 "To find out how much Bart spent to buy the tickets, we need to multiply $\\[math]12.69$ by $4$.\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.6\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}4; 5\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 7\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\nThen, we insert the decimal point in the result to have the same number of decimal places as the decimal factor.\n\nThere are $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places in $12.69$, so the product will also have ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ decimal places. We take our value of $5076$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $\\color{blue}2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n50\\,\\overset{\\color{red}\\downarrow}{\\color{red}\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{.}}\\!\\!\\!\\underbrace{76}_{\\large\\text{[/math]\\color{blue}2[math] digits}}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n12.69 \\times 4 = 50.76 \\,\n$$\n\nTherefore, Bart spent $\\[/math]50.76$ to buy the tickets." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138216 1069 "e-14536" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138216 1071 "What is $34.1 \\times 52.2$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138216 1072 #uuid "72b93987-21c0-5dea-99f8-5cadd967596b" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138216 1074 "First, we ignore the decimal point and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}2[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math] \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}.1 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.2; 6\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 2 \\\\ + & 6\\end{vmatrix}8\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ + & 1\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 8 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 2\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $34.1$ and there is $\\color{blue}1$ decimal place in $52.2$, so their product will have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}} + {\\color{blue}{1}} = 2\n$$\n\ndecimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $178002$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n34.1 \\times 52.2 = 1780.02 \\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138217 1069 "e-1526" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138217 1071 "Density $D$ is the rate that mass $M$ changes as volume $V$ changes. What are the units of density in terms of base units?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138217 1072 #uuid "f0782388-8184-5922-8e9e-d9edcf727264" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138217 1074 "We *measure the change* in the mass, as *we change* the volume. Consequently, any appropriate unit must take the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{unit of mass}}{\\textrm{unit of volume}}\n$$\n\nThe base unit for mass is $\\textrm{kg}$, and the base unit for volume is $\\textrm{m}^3$. Therefore, the units of density are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{m}^3}\n$$\n\nor, kilograms per cubic meter." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138218 1069 "e-15666" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138218 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing digits in the following multiplication problem?\n\n$4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 6 \\\\ 2 & [math]\\phantom{i}[/math]\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\phantom{i}[/math] \\mid 4$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138218 1072 #uuid "a3149535-f4fd-5e7c-992b-db326209eb96" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138218 1074 "First, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n_(5); 4 \\mid 7\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}6; 4 \\mid 9 \\cdot 6 = 54; Carry:5; Write:4\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\left._(4)\\right|_(5); 4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 6 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{vmatrix}7 \\cdot 6 = 42and5 + 42 = 47; Carry:4; Write:7\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the hundreds:\n\n$$\n\\left._(4)\\right|_(5); 4\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 6 \\\\ 2 & [math]\\color{red}8[/math]\\end{vmatrix}7 \\mid 4 \\mid 4 \\cdot 6 = 24and4 + 24 = 28; Carry:-; Write:28\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing digits, from left-to-right, are $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}$ and $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138219 1069 "e-15667" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138219 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing digits in the following multiplication problem?\n\n$1\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\phantom{i}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}9 & [math]\\phantom{i}[/math]\\end{vmatrix}4$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138219 1072 #uuid "4ded161c-a702-57fe-83ed-5389fec1b3ba" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138219 1074 "First, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n_(1); 1\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}2 \\cdot 7 = 14; Carry:1; Write:4\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\left._(3)\\right|_(1); 1\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 6\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 5 \\cdot 7 = 35and1 + 35 = 36; Carry:3; Write:6\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the hundreds:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\lef\\right|._(5) \\mid _(3)\\right|_(1); 1\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 9\\end{vmatrix}6 \\mid 4 \\mid 8 \\cdot 7 = 56and3 + 56 = 59; Carry:5; Write:9\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the thousands:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\lef\\right|._(5) \\mid _(3)\\right|_(1); 1\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}2; \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}7 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\color{red}2[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 6 & 4\\end{vmatrix}1 \\cdot 7 = 7and5 + 7 = 12; Carry:-; Write:12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing digits, from left-to-right, are $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$ and $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138220 1069 "e-1589" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138220 1071 "For the number $984$, which digit is in the tens place?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138220 1072 #uuid "16e93433-c5e7-5f9f-b2f0-5aded525d916" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138220 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values.\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $9$ | $\\color{blue}8$ | $4$ |\n\nSo in the tens place, we have $8$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138221 1069 "e-1590" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138221 1071 "In $5,372$, which digit is in the thousands place?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138221 1072 #uuid "4eb52f48-376e-59ef-a1b8-008840c29010" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138221 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values.\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| ${\\color{blue}{5}}$ | $3$ | $7$ | $2$ |\n\nSo in the thousands place, we have $5$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138222 1069 "e-1591" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138222 1071 "What number has $5$ thousands, $1$ ten, and $2$ ones?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138222 1072 #uuid "1ad5db29-6aa4-5eef-a72d-9e4850e5d35f" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138222 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values. Notice that we have zero hundreds.\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nSo, our number is $5,012$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138223 1069 "e-1656" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138223 1071 "In $21.73$, which digit is in the tenths place?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138223 1072 #uuid "cfcc44f6-325f-5466-9c60-c4ab1c09639d" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138223 1074 "Let's write down the place values:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $1$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}7$ | $3$ |\n\nIn the tenths place, we have ${\\color{blue}{7}}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138224 1069 "e-1657" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138224 1071 "Which digit is in the hundredths place in $528.60$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138224 1072 #uuid "99807b8f-747a-50f6-80ba-6324e5ea6e0e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138224 1074 "Let's write down the place values:\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $2$ | $8$ | **.** | $6$ | $\\color{blue}0$ |\n\nIn the hundredths place, we have ${\\color{blue}{0}}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138225 1069 "e-1658" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138225 1071 "Complete the statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ The number $98.654$ has $4$ $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{55555555555}}}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138225 1072 #uuid "19aa3332-c42b-5aad-86b9-2b637342f4ee" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138225 1074 "Let's write down the place values:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $9$ | $8$ | **.** | $6$ | $5$ | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n\nWe have $\\color{blue}4$ in thousandths place, so the complete statement is \"The number $98.654$ has $4$ thousandths\"." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138226 1069 "e-1659" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138226 1071 "What number has $8$ ten thousands, $5$ tens, and $1$ thousandths?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138226 1072 #uuid "baf29150-d8ab-5a0e-aaa5-7d1d3ab81608" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138226 1074 "Let's write down the place values. We have $8$ ten thousands, $5$ tens, and $1$ thousandths. For the other place values, we just write zero.\n\n| **ten thousands** | **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $8$ | $0$ | $0$ | $5$ | $0$ | **.** | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nSo, $80,050.001$ is our number." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138227 1069 "e-1660" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138227 1071 "How many times greater is the value of the digit $6$ in $2.361$ than the value of the digit $6$ in $2.856$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138227 1072 #uuid "879f9389-18e6-5a39-b3b2-0f3095d69715" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138227 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values for $2.3{\\color{blue}6}1$.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | **.** | $3$ | $\\color{blue}6$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we write the place values for $2.85{\\color{red}6}$.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | **.** | $8$ | $5$ | $\\color{red}6$ |\n\nTo compare the two digits, we move $1$ step to the left in the place value chart. Each step to the left is an *increase* by a factor of $10$:\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | **.** |  | $\\color{blue}6$ | $\\color{red}6$ |\n|  | ![](images/fd/fdbcaa2c29c81fcdd77d65d1ef3096c1ae46a87d0d8d392e7e6f947a24da86cc.png) |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, the value of the digit $\\color{blue}6$ in the hundredths place is $10$ times greater than the value of the digit $\\color{red}6$ in the thousandths place." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138228 1069 "e-1661" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138228 1071 "Find a number in which the digit $9$ has a value that is $1,000$ times larger than the value of the digit $9$ in $313.9$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138228 1072 #uuid "a9b04529-aa8f-5f89-bf94-bce73d12830f" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138228 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values for $313.9$.\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $3$ | **.** | $\\color{red}9$ |\n\nWe need to find a digit that is $1,000$ times greater than this ${\\color{red}{9}}$.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n1,000 = \\underbrace{10\\times 10\\times 10}_{3\\,\\text{steps}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we move this digit $\\color{red}9$ three steps to the left in the place value chart. This gives:\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}9$ |  |  | **.** |  |\n\nThus, the required number is *any* number that has the digit $\\color{blue}9$ in the hundreds place. Some examples include:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}9}15.74, \\qquad 2,{\\color{blue}9}80.2, \\qquad 32,{\\color{blue}9}01.56\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138229 1069 "e-1662" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138229 1071 "How many times smaller is the digit $\\color{blue}6$ in $11.{\\color{blue}6}2$ than the digit $\\color{red}6$ in ${\\color{red}6}.751$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138229 1072 #uuid "2357d772-a878-5528-abf6-54c6cb33fe58" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138229 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values for $11.{\\color{red}6}2$.\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $1$ | **.** | $\\color{red}6$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNow we write the place values for ${\\color{blue}6}.751$.\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $\\color{blue}6$ | **.** | $7$ | $5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow let's jump from the ones place to the tenths place.\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $\\color{blue}6$ | **.** | $\\color{red}6$ |  |  |\n| ![](images/ac/ac3143a3744afa4aef1927288253cf79333bc615b7d07435663170866995180c.png) |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, the value of the digit $\\color{red}6$ in the tenths place is $10$ times smaller than the value of the digit $\\color{blue}6$ in the ones place.\n\nAlternatively, we can also say that the value of the digit $\\color{red}6$ in the tenths place is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nthe value of the digit $\\color{blue}6$ in the ones place." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138230 1069 "e-1663" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138230 1071 "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\n$\\qquad$ The digit $4$ in $57.934$ is $\\underline{\\phantom{{}^{000000000000000000000000000}}}$ the value of the digit $4$ in $0.416$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138230 1072 #uuid "ac5c3a77-7e52-56bd-bde9-6b57b272420e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138230 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values for $57.93{\\color{red}{4}}$.\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $7$ | **.** | $9$ | $3$ | $\\color{red}4$ |\n\nThen, we write the place values for $0.{\\color{blue}{4}}16$.\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $0$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}4$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n\nNow let's jump from the tenths place to the thousandths place.\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | **.** | $\\color{red}4$ |  | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n| ![](images/a4/a4cba647f534ded9dfd9d7aca38eca61cfac031b8749790c0866ce118d2488fa.png) |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nWe see that the value of the digit $4$ in the thousandths place is\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{10\\times 10}_{\\color{black}2\\,\\text{steps}}=100\n$$\n\ntimes **smaller** than the value of the digit $4$ in the tenths place.\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is \"The digit $4$ in $57.934$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{100}\n$$\n\nthe value of the digit $4$ in $0.416$.\"" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138231 1069 "e-1691" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138231 1071 "Is the following statement true?\n\n$7.2< 7.02$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138231 1072 #uuid "3682e7b0-9d95-53eb-bb13-469834775705" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138231 1074 "We compare $7.2$ and $7.02$ using a place value chart.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}2$ | $0$ |\n| $7$ | **.** | $\\color{red}0$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now compare the place values of both numbers, going *from left to right*.\n\n- Looking at the digits in the ones place, we see that they are the same for both numbers ($7$).\n- Moving on to the tenths place, we see that the digit $\\color{red}0$ is less than the digit $\\color{blue}2$. Using the \"less than\" symbol, we can write ${\\color{red}{0}} < {\\color{blue}{2}}$.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n7.{\\color{red}{0}}2< 7.{\\color{blue}{2}}0\n$$\n\nSo, the original statement is *false*." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138232 1069 "e-1692" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138232 1071 "From the digits $3,7$ and $8$, which of them will make the following statement true?\n\n$0$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138232 1072 #uuid "9f88a5a2-8504-5ae9-a7eb-d2210774fcea" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138232 1074 "Let's write down the place value charts for both numbers.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | **.** | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| $7$ | **.** | $1$ | $\\color{blue}6$ | $0$ |\n\nWe then compare place values, going *from left to right.*\n\n- The digit in the ones place is the same for both numbers ($7$).\n- Moving onto the tenths place, we see that the digits are also the same ($1$).\n- Moving on to the hundredths place, we see that the first number is less than the second if $0$ is less than $\\color{blue}6$.\n\nFrom the given answer choices, only $\\color{red}3$ is less than $\\color{blue}6$. Therefore, only $\\color{red}3$ will make the statement true." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138233 1069 "e-1693" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138233 1071 "From the digits $1,6$ and $7$, which of them will make the following statement true?\n\n$0$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138233 1072 #uuid "f5a5e1c9-1bcd-5c53-b474-27e82c37bf81" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138233 1074 "Let's write down the place value charts for both numbers.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}5$ | $1$ |\n| $0$ | **.** | $0$ | $7$ |\n\nWe now compare place values, going from left to right.\n\n- The digits in the ones place are the same for both numbers.\n- Moving on to the tenths place, we see that the first number is greater than the second if $\\color{blue}5$ is greater than $0$\n\nFrom the given answer choices, $\\color{blue}5$ is greater than $\\color{red}1$ only. Therefore, only $\\color{red}1$ makes the statement true." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138234 1069 "e-1694" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138234 1071 "The table below shows the prices of five grocery items.\n\n|  | Price |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Bread | $\\[math] 0.69$ |\n| Cheese | $\\[/math] 3.20$ |\n| Tomato | $\\[math] 1$ |\n| Ketchup | $\\[/math] 0.53$ |\n| Eggs | $\\[math] 1.98$ |\n\nIf we want to discard the most expensive item, which one should we reject?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138234 1072 #uuid "2acfb461-fedb-5201-9332-a1e633806b31" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138234 1074 "We write down all the prices in a place value chart.\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | **.** | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $\\color{blue}3$ | **.** | $2$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | **.** | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $0$ | **.** | $5$ | $3$ |\n| $1$ | **.** | $9$ | $8$ |\n\nWe then compare place values, going from left to right.\n\nWe note that for the digits in the ones place, the largest digit in this position is ${\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nTherefore, $\\[math] {\\color{blue}3}.20$ is the price of the most expensive item. So, we should discard the cheese." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138235 1069 "e-1705" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138235 1071 "If the speed of an object is measured in $\\textrm{mm/min}$, what are the units of its acceleration?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138235 1072 #uuid "51b8e09e-18d4-5879-ad6c-a7ee83157115" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138235 1074 "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\textrm{mm/min}$ and the time is measured in $\\textrm{min}$.\n\n![](images/ff/fffb2fc8208fa89a69bfefd02cb97feecbbe3175411e420dfe47cc7a8ae3bd23.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\dfrac{\\textrm{change in speed}}{\\textrm{change in time}}\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{units of speed}}{\\textrm{units of time}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mm/min}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{min}} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138236 1069 "e-1706" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138236 1071 "A fly accelerates with constant acceleration from $20\\, \\textrm{m/min}$ to $100\\, \\textrm{m/min}$ in $2$ minutes. Find its acceleration." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138236 1072 #uuid "d1875220-7af4-5d5c-a0dc-93b1843aa4e0" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138236 1074 "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. To plot a speed-time graph, we need two points.\n\n- The fly starts at a speed of $20 \\, \\textrm{m/min}$. This corresponds to the point $(0,20)$.\n- In $2$ minutes, the fly has reached a speed of $100 \\, \\textrm{m/min}$. This corresponds to the point $(2,100)$.\n\nWe plot the two points and draw a line through them as follows:\n\n![](images/91/914d4c32971627058b8e8261e26a8d84de89657e948a6243d4f41f51f60b79a8.png)\n\nThe slope of the fly's speed-time graph gives its acceleration. The slope of this line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{100m/min - 20m/min}{2min - 0min} \\\\\n&= \\frac{80m/min}{2min} \\\\\n&= 40\\frac{m}{min^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration of the fly is $40 \\, \\textrm{m/min}^2$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138237 1069 "e-1707" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138237 1071 "The speed-time graph shown below represents the motion of a train. At what times is the train decelerating?\n\n![](images/e7/e755bb849bdfbb9fd4057330ed5374fea0c1fc4a6f914a0a058635181aef2b10.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138237 1072 #uuid "314ed8cf-b4cf-5b4a-b168-8dcbaf1a928f" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138237 1074 "The train is decelerating when its acceleration is negative. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is negative.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is negative from\n\n$$\nt = 15\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nto\n\n$$\nt = 40\\,\\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the train is decelerating in this time interval." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138238 1069 "e-1708" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138238 1071 "A car accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $8 \\, \\textrm{m/s}^2$ for the first $10$ seconds of its motion. Then the car decelerates with a constant deceleration of $16 \\, \\textrm{m/s}^2$ until it stops. What graph represents the described motion?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138238 1072 #uuid "ffd579d3-18eb-5204-9cf1-8c8188bfa1e7" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138238 1074 "We start the first line from the point $(0,0)$, since the car is starting from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $8 \\, \\textrm{m/s}^2$, we draw a line with slope $8$.\n\nHowever, because the car is only accelerating for the first $10$ seconds, we stop the line at $t=10$. At this time, the car's speed has increased by\n\n$$\n8 \\cdot 10 = 80 \\textrm{m/s}\n$$\n\nSo, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(10,80)$.\n\n![](images/8d/8ded6c68c30659a4943c4b2df5a390a151efe03217ee2b4927513cb2ed3d6602.png)\n\nAfter $t=10$, the car has a deceleration of $16 \\, \\textrm{m/s}^2$, so the slope of the second line segment is $-16$. The second line segment stops when the car's speed returns to $0$. In order to figure out when this is, we need to find the equation for the second line segment.\n\nSince the second line segment passes through the point $(10, 80)$ and has a slope of $-16$, we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv - v_{1} &= m(t - t_{1}) \\\\\nv - 80 &= -16(t - 10) \\\\\nv - 80 &= -16t + 160 \\\\\nv &= -16t + 240\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve for $t$ when the speed of the car is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= -16t + 240 \\\\\n0 &= -16t + 240 \\\\\n16t &= 240 \\\\\nt &= \\frac{240}{16} \\\\\nt &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second line segment stops at the point $(15,0)$. The final graph is\n\n![](images/12/12865966498569211a7940b3e77f22875e2f98803f50472f1a91469fbdfb5cbf.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138239 1069 "e-1711" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138239 1071 "What is $268$ multiplied by $54$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138239 1072 #uuid "c0d305a1-67a3-581a-898c-27a4c3520d98" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138239 1074 "We write it this way:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 8 \\\\ \\cdot & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix}1, 072 + 13, 400 = 14, 472\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}2[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}3[/math] \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix}268 \\cdot 4 = 1, 0720000000\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}3[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}2[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}4[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}3[/math] \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 268 \\cdot 50 = 13, 4000000\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}3[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}2[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}4[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}3[/math] \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ + & 1 & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix}1, 072 + 13, 400 = 14, 472\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n268 \\times 54 = 14, 472\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138240 1069 "e-1712" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138240 1071 "A hospital orders $21$ shipments of syringes. Each shipment contains $4,859$ syringes. How many syringes has the hospital ordered in total?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138240 1072 #uuid "5218e366-f530-5bf2-ac5c-c0a232580269" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138240 1074 "To calculate the total number of syringes the hospital ordered, we need to multiply $4,859$ by $21$.\n\nWe write it this way:\n\n$$\n4\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}4, 859 + 97, 180 = 102, 039\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math] & [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math] \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}1; 4\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 & 9\\end{vmatrix}4, 859 \\cdot 1 = 4, 8590000000\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}1[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; 4\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}9; 9\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & 8 & 0\\end{vmatrix}4, 859 \\cdot 20 = 97, 18000000\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}1[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; 4\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 5\\end{vmatrix}9; +\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 7 & 1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}9 \\mid 4, 859 + 97, 180 = 102, 039\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n4,859 \\times 21 = 102, 039\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hospital ordered $102,039$ syringes in total." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138241 1069 "e-1713" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138241 1071 "From left to right, what are the missing digits in the following multiplication problem?\n$4\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 & 8 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & [math]\\phantom{i}[/math] & 0\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\phantom{i}[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}3 & [math]\\phantom{i}[/math]\\end{vmatrix}3$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138241 1072 #uuid "4d73eba1-8f5b-5892-8208-a42baa5da65c" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138241 1074 "We write it this way:\n\n$$\n4\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 & 8 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}42, 689 \\cdot 40 = 1, 707, 560\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}4[/math] & [math]\\color{blue}6[/math] & [math]\\color{blue}6[/math] \\\\ 4 & 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}8\\begin{vmatrix}9 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 9 & 8 & 8 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}42, 689 \\cdot 7 = 298, 82300\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}1[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}2[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}4[/math]\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}3[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}6[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}3[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}6[/math] \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 8 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 9 & 8 & 8\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 7 & 5 & 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}42, 689 \\cdot 40 = 1, 707, 560\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}1[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}2[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}4[/math]\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}3[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}6[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}3[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}6[/math] \\\\ 4\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 8 & 9 \\\\ \\cdot & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 9 & 8 & 8\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 0 & 7 & 5 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & [math]0[/math] & 0 & [math]6[/math] & 3 & [math]8[/math]\\end{vmatrix}3 \\mid 298, 823 + 1, 707, 560 = 0,,; = 2, 006, 383\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing digits are $0,6$ and $8$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138242 1069 "e-1714" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138242 1071 "Find the value of $2,324\\times 46$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138242 1072 #uuid "cc672e12-92e2-54c1-9f7e-9a5a2f22d3e3" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138242 1074 "We write it this way:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ \\cdot & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}6\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}1[/math] & [math]\\color{blue}2[/math] \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 & 4 & 4\\end{vmatrix}2, 324 \\cdot 6 = 13, 944\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}1[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}1[/math]\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}2[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ \\cdot & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}2, 324 \\cdot 40 = 92, 960\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}1[/math]\\begin{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math] \\\\ [math]\\color{blue}1[/math]\\end{vmatrix}[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}2[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ \\cdot & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}9\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 6 & 9\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 4 \\mid 13, 944 + 92, 960 = 106, 904\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2,324 \\times 46 = 106, 904\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138243 1069 "e-1736" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138243 1071 "Estimate $15.123 + 8.8$ by rounding each number to the nearest whole number." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138243 1072 #uuid "9182c486-e146-5ee9-83d0-557f61c41a0e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138243 1074 "First, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$15.123\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 8.8 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 15 & +\\end{vmatrix}9$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}15} + {\\color{blue}9} = 24\n$$\n\nTherefore, $15.123 + 8.8$ is about $24$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138244 1069 "e-1737" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138244 1071 "Joe cuts a piece of wire into two lengths. One part is $8.15$ meters long, and the other part is $7.75$ meters long. By rounding each number to the nearest whole number, estimate the length of the wire before Joe cut it." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138244 1072 #uuid "34d8e729-0ba9-50a0-a611-45aa8785d2a7" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138244 1074 "To estimate the initial length of the wire, we need to estimate the sum $8.15+ 7.75$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$8.15\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 7.75 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 8 & +\\end{vmatrix}8$\n\nNext, we calculate the sum:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}8} + {\\color{blue}8} = 16\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the wire was approximately $16$ meters before Joe cut it." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138245 1069 "e-1738" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138245 1071 "By rounding each number to the nearest whole number, estimate the value of $55.1 - 24.76$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138245 1072 #uuid "dd158243-172f-5a0d-a652-09db8e36f824" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138245 1074 "First, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$55.1\\begin{vmatrix}- & 24.76 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 55 & -\\end{vmatrix}25$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}55} - {\\color{blue}25} = 30\n$$\n\nTherefore, $55.1 - 24.76$ is approximately $30$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138246 1069 "e-1739" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138246 1071 "A meteorologist measures the total amount of rainfall that two cities, city $A$ and city $B$, received in a week. He found that both cities received a combined total of $14.7$ inches of rain. If city $A$ received $5.745$ inches of rain, then by rounding each number to the nearest whole number, estimate the amount of rainfall that city $B$ received." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138246 1072 #uuid "e83b6318-a509-5685-b608-6b5cde65e449" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138246 1074 "To estimate the total amount of rain that city $B$ received, we need to estimate the difference $14.7 - 5.745$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest whole number:\n$14.7\\begin{vmatrix}- & 5.745 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 15 & -\\end{vmatrix}6$\n\nNext, we calculate the difference:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}15} - {\\color{blue}6} = 9\n$$\n\nTherefore, city $B$ received approximately $9$ inches of rainfall during the week." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138247 1069 "e-1922" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138247 1071 "Write $3.729$ in expanded form." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138247 1072 #uuid "0e74cfe9-10f5-57d3-9450-18d5895381a7" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138247 1074 "We start by writing down the place values:\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | **.** | $7$ | $2$ | $9$ |\n\nNow, to write the number in expanded form, we multiply each digit by its place value and add them up:\n\n$$\n(3\\times 1) + \\left(7\\times \\dfrac{1}{10}\\right) + \\left(2\\times\\dfrac{1}{100}\\right) + \\left(9\\times\\dfrac{1}{1,000}\\right)\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138248 1069 "e-1925" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138248 1071 "Write $6.42$ in expanded form." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138248 1072 #uuid "1e1d9b5d-c5a1-5da1-bf70-5dc93012326e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138248 1074 "We need to convert the given number from standard form to expanded form.\n\nLet's start by writing down the place values of $6.42$:\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $6$ | **.** | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nFrom the table:\n\n- $6$ ones is $6$, which we can write as $6\\times 1$\n- $4$ tenths is $0.4$, which we can write as $4\\times 0.1$\n- $2$ hundredths is $0.02$, which we can write as $2\\times 0.01$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n6.42 = 6\\times 1 + 4\\times 0.1 + 2\\times 0.01\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138249 1069 "e-1927" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138249 1071 "Write $81.303$ in expanded form." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138249 1072 #uuid "f7ed79a1-3d19-5e76-8a5f-b24299160fc1" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138249 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values.\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $8$ | $1$ | **.** | $3$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nNow, to write the number in expanded form, we multiply each digit by its place value and add them up:\n\n$$\n81.303 = (8\\times10) + ({1 \\times 1}) + \\left(3 \\times\\dfrac{1}{10}\\right) +\\left(0 \\times\\dfrac{1}{100}\\right) +\\left(3 \\times\\dfrac{1}{1,000}\\right)\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n0 \\times\\dfrac{1}{100} = 0\n$$\n\nwe can simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n81.303 = (8\\times10) + ({1 \\times 1}) + \\left(3 \\times\\dfrac{1}{10}\\right) +\\left(3 \\times\\dfrac{1}{1,000}\\right)\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138250 1069 "e-1928" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138250 1071 "Which number is equivalent to the expanded form below?\n\n$(5\\times 10) + (3\\times 1) + \\left(8\\times\\dfrac{1}{10}\\right)+\\left(7\\times\\dfrac{1}{100}\\right)$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138250 1072 #uuid "b58ef040-d00b-5500-8b1c-f092620b1601" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138250 1074 "We need to convert the given number from expanded form to standard form. First, notice that:\n\n$\\qquad$ $5\\times 10$ is $5$ tens.\n\n$\\qquad$ $3\\times 1$ is $3$ ones.\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n8\\times\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nis $8$ tenths.\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n7\\times\\dfrac{1}{100}\n$$\n\nis $7$ hundredths.\n\nPutting this into the place value chart, we get:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $3$ | **.** | $8$ | $7$ |\n\nTherefore, the given expanded form is equivalent to $53.87$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138251 1069 "e-1929" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138251 1071 "Which number is equivalent to the expanded form below?\n\n$2\\times 10 + 4\\times 1 + 1\\times 0.1 + 8\\times 0.01$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138251 1072 #uuid "4337e331-4089-583f-81b8-2a9d112d7412" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138251 1074 "We need to convert the given number from expanded form to standard form. First, notice that:\n\n$\\qquad$ $2\\times 10$ is $2$ tens.\n\n$\\qquad$ $4\\times 1$ is $4$ ones.\n\n$\\qquad$ $1\\times 0.1$ is $1$ tenth.\n\n$\\qquad$ $8\\times 0.01$ is $8$ hundredths.\n\nPutting this into a place value chart, we get:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $4$ | **.** | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nTherefore, the given expanded form is equivalent to $24.18$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138252 1069 "e-1930" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138252 1071 "What are the missing digits in the following equality?\n\n$0$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138252 1072 #uuid "3da8c281-b3f4-5972-8edb-13a9794b71d3" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138252 1074 "We need to convert the given number from expanded form to standard form. First, notice that:\n\n$\\qquad$ $70$ is $7$ tens.\n\n$\\qquad$ $8$ is $8$ ones.\n\n$\\qquad$ $0.04$ is $4$ hundredths.\n\n$\\qquad$ $0.002$ is $2$ thousandths.\n\nThere are no tenths in the given expression which means that we have $0$ tenths.\n\nPutting this into the place value chart, we get:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $8$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}0$ | $4$ | $\\color{blue}2$ |\n\nTherefore, the resulting number is $78.042$ and the missing digits are $0$ and $2$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138253 1069 "e-1931" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138253 1071 "Which number is equivalent to the expanded form below?\n\n$(5\\times 1,000) + \\dfrac{1}{1,000}$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138253 1072 #uuid "72d9ef29-c7ac-5301-ba10-f629846ede75" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138253 1074 "We need to convert the given number from expanded form to standard form. We notice that:\n\n$\\qquad$ $5\\times 1,000$ is $5$ thousands.\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1,000}\n$$\n\nis $1$ thousandth.\n\nThere are no hundreds, tens, ones, tenths and hundredths in the given expression, which means that we have $0$ hundreds, $0$ tens, $0$ ones, $0$ tenths, and $0$ hundredths.\nPutting this into the place value chart, we get:\n\n| **thousands** | **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | **.** | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, the given expanded form is equivalent to $5,000.001$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138254 1069 "e-2014" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138254 1071 "Beth calculates $12.5 \\div 10$ and places her answer in the table below. From **left to right**, what are the missing digits?\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | **.** | $2$ | $0$ |" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138254 1072 #uuid "a13fd2af-adf5-5201-a0e7-1d85b0de43fc" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138254 1074 "First, we write down the place value chart for $12.5$:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $2$ | **.** | $5$ |  |\n\nThen, we move all digits by one place to the *right.*\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{1}$ | **.** | $2$ | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{5}$ |\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n12.5\\div 10 = 1.25\\,\n$$\n\nThe missing digits are therefore $1$ and $5$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138255 1069 "e-2015" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138255 1071 "Find the place value chart of $225 \\div 10$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138255 1072 #uuid "df116d9b-f52f-55ca-82fa-8b0e49934cd5" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138255 1074 "First, we write down the place value chart for $225$:\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $5$ | **.** |  |\n\nThen, we move all digits by one place to the *right.*\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $2$ | $2$ | **.** | $5$ |" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138256 1069 "e-2016" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138256 1071 "What is the missing digit in the place value chart below for $0.3\\div 100$?\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | **.** | $0$ | $0$ | $3$ |" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138256 1072 #uuid "35ac27fb-5580-5337-a75a-53b3a656a88d" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138256 1074 "First, we write down the place value chart for $0.3$:\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | **.** | $3$ |  |  |\n\nThen, we determine the number of places to move. We're dividing by $100$, which has $2$ zeros, so we move all values to the *right* by $2$ places:\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | **.** |  | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nFinally, we put zeros in all empty cells that are to the right of the decimal point, and also in the empty ones place:\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | **.** | $0$ | $\\bbox[3pt, lightgray]{0}$ | $3$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.3\\div 100 = 0.003 \\,\n$$\n\nThe missing digit is $0$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138257 1069 "e-2017" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138257 1071 "From **left 