{red}1$ is less than the divisor $5$. But we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,\\,\\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}}$$5$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}} {\\color{blue}0}}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{lightgray}0$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n| $\\,\\,\\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\,. \\,} 2$$5$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\,. \\,} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$5$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n46 \\div 5 = 9.2\\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138259 1069 "e-2044" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138259 1071 "Find $51 \\div 4$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138259 1072 #uuid "8df0cb73-298a-59a7-ab3a-3ecd0b0e186f" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138259 1074 "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2$$4$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$1$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{red}3$ |\n| --- |\n\nNotice that the remainder $\\color{red}3$ is less than the divisor $4$, but we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}}$$4$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}} {\\color{blue}0}}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$1$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}3$$\\color{lightgray}0$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, adding more zeros as needed but stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n| $\\,\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\,. \\,} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5$$4$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$1$$1$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n51 \\div 4 = 12.75\\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138260 1069 "e-2045" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138260 1071 "What is $79 \\div 8$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138260 1072 #uuid "eecb5bae-b48f-557c-a2d5-4fc12c24079a" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138260 1074 "We start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\phantom{0}{\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9$$8$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$${\\color{red}7}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNotice that the remainder $\\color{red}7$ is less than the divisor $8$, but we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,\\,\\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}}$$8$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}} {\\color{blue}0}}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}7$$\\color{lightgray}0$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, adding more zeros as needed but stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n| $\\,\\,{\\phantom{0}} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\,. \\,} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5$$8$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 9 {\\,. \\,} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$7$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$6$$4$$6$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$5$$6$$4$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n79 \\div 8 = 9.875\\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138261 1069 "e-2046" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138261 1071 "Cameron spent $\\[math]607$ in five days. If she spent an equal amount each day, how much did Cameron spend per day?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138261 1072 #uuid "8e2daf0a-34d6-590a-92a2-913ec6fed435" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138261 1074 "To determine the amount Cameron spent each day, we need to divide $607$ by $5$.\n\nWe start by going through the long division procedure as usual:\n\n| $\\,\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1$$5$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$0$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\color{red}2$ |\n| --- |\n\nNotice that the remainder $\\color{red}2$ is less than the divisor $5$. But we can proceed further by adding a $\\color{blue}0$ after the decimal point in the dividend. We also add a decimal point in the quotient, as shown below:\n\n| $\\,\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}}$$5$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7{\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}}\\color{blue}0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$1$$0$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$0$$7$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$$\\color{red}2$$\\color{lightgray}0$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow we complete the division, as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0$:\n\n| $\\,\\,1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 1 {\\,.\\,} 4$$5$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7 {\\,.\\,} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$0$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$2$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$\\fbox{0}$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n607 \\div 5 = 121.4 \\,\n$$\n\nWe conclude that Cameron spent $\\[math]121.40$ each day." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138262 1069 "e-2056" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138262 1071 "Find the value of $10^3$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138262 1072 #uuid "d811160a-b9f5-500d-ac11-0abb2c89685b" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138262 1074 "The number $10^3$ is in exponent form where the base is $10$ and the exponent is $3$. The exponent tells us how many zeros to include in the result.\n\nSo, we write $1$ followed by a block of $3$ zeros:\n\n$$\n3\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138263 1069 "e-2058" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138263 1071 "What is the missing exponent?\n$0$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138263 1072 #uuid "ac8fdd0f-4993-5ab4-b682-8db31780ac43" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138263 1074 "First, notice that $10$ on the right-hand side has a block of $1$ zero:\n$1 \\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\underset{\\substack{\\uparrow \\[2pt] \\large \\text{one zero}}}{\\color{blue}0}\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$\n\nThe exponent corresponds to the number of zeros. Therefore, the missing exponent is ${\\color{red}1}$, and\n\n$$\n10^{\\color{red}1}=10\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138264 1069 "e-2059" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138264 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $1,000,000,000$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138264 1072 #uuid "4fc980c3-1477-5b99-9d35-00908ad7c6cd" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138264 1074 "First, notice that $1,000,000,000$ has a block of $9$ zeros:\n$9$\n\nWe can write the number in exponent form where the exponent corresponds to the number of zeros:\n\n$$\n10^{\\color{red}9}=1,000,000,000\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138265 1069 "e-2064" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138265 1071 "Find an expression equivalent to $9.8 \\times 10^{2}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138265 1072 #uuid "7c2b1550-6079-5bc6-af21-c1be55018883" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138265 1074 "First, we rewrite $10^2$ as $1$ followed by a block of $2$ zeros:\n$2$\n\nSince we're multiplying by $100$ and $100$ has $\\color{blue} 2$ zeros, we move the decimal point $\\color{blue}2$ places to the right:\n\n$$\n9.8 \\rightarrow 980.0 = 980\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n9.8 \\times 10^{2} = 980 \\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138266 1069 "e-2065" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138266 1071 "What is the value of $0.61 \\times 10^{3}$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138266 1072 #uuid "f3cc4c73-860e-509e-bf3c-b726c6cc346c" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138266 1074 "First, we rewrite $10^3$ as $1$ followed by a block of $3$ zeros:\n$3$\n\nSince we're multiplying by $1,000$ and $1,000$ has $\\color{blue} 3$ zeros, we move the decimal point $\\color{blue}3$ places to the right:\n\n$$\n0.61 \\rightarrow 610.0 = 610\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n0.61 \\times 10^{3} = 610\\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138267 1069 "e-2066" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138267 1071 "Find the value of $8.046 \\times 10^{2}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138267 1072 #uuid "686f095d-c551-52e5-afea-7ac603c9429d" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138267 1074 "First, we rewrite $10^2$ as $1$ followed by a block of $2$ zeros:\n$2$\n\nSince we're multiplying by $100$ and $100$ has $\\color{blue} 2$ zeros, we move the decimal point $\\color{blue}2$ places to the right:\n\n$$\n8.046 \\rightarrow 804.6\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n8.046 \\times 10^{2} = 804.6 \\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138268 1069 "e-2067" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138268 1071 "$7.58 \\times 10^{5} =$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138268 1072 #uuid "1d7f460b-0f9b-587d-8e52-056822769070" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138268 1074 "First, we rewrite $10^5$ as $1$ followed by a block of $5$ zeros:\n$5$\n\nSince we're multiplying by $100,000$ and $100,000$ has $\\color{blue} 5$ zeros, we move the decimal point $\\color{blue}5$ places to the right:\n\n$$\n7.58 \\rightarrow 758000.0 = 758000\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n7.58 \\times 10^{5} = 758,000 \\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138269 1069 "e-2183" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138269 1071 "A paddling pool with a capacity of $997$ gallons is filled through a pump that provides $19$ gallons of water every minute. By rounding each number to the nearest ten, estimate the number of minutes needed to fill the pool." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138269 1072 #uuid "c40de392-334c-5b74-a45a-c2c1f5e53224" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138269 1074 "Here, we need to estimate the value of $997 \\div 19$.\n\nFirst, we round both numbers to the nearest ten:\n$997\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 19 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 1, 000 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}20$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $1,000 \\div 20$, which can be written as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,000}{20}\n$$\n\nBy dividing the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1,000 \\div 10}{\\, \\, \\, \\, \\,\\, \\, \\, 20 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{100}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as\n\n$$\n100 \\div 2 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, $997 \\div 19$ is about $50$.\n\nSo, it will take about $50$ minutes to fill the pool." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138270 1069 "e-2184" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138270 1071 "Estimate the value of $52,284 \\div 841$ by rounding the dividend to the nearest thousand and rounding the divisor to the nearest hundred." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138270 1072 #uuid "fc909434-3d93-5ae0-9c4d-ea7531f0329b" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138270 1074 "We round the dividend to the nearest thousand:\n$52,284 \\rightarrow 52,000$\n\nAlso, we round the divisor to the nearest hundred:\n$841 \\rightarrow 800$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $52,000 \\div 800$, which can be written as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{52,000}{800}\n$$\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $100$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{52,000 \\div 100}{800 \\div 100} = \\dfrac{520}{8}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as $520 \\div 8$.\n\nComputing the quotient, we obtain:\n\n| $\\,\\,\\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5$$8\\,$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$4$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $52,284 \\div 841$ is approximately $65$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138271 1069 "e-2185" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138271 1071 "By rounding both numbers to the nearest ten, estimate the value of $727 \\div 22$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138271 1072 #uuid "db1f2a1f-ca39-51ef-b20e-cda4c009728d" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138271 1074 "First, we round both numbers to the nearest ten:\n$727\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 22 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 730 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}20$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $730 \\div 20$, which we write as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{730}{20}\n$$\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{730 \\div 10}{20 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{73}{2}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as $73 \\div 2$.\n\nComputing the quotient, we obtain:\n\n| $\\,\\,3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\,. \\,} 5$$2$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\,. \\,} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$1$$3$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$1$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $727 \\div 22$ is approximately $36.5 \\,$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138272 1069 "e-2186" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138272 1071 "By rounding both numbers to the nearest hundred, estimate the value of $142,773 \\div 398$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138272 1072 #uuid "687e020d-c7ee-5fed-8775-4290c560382c" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138272 1074 "We round both numbers to the nearest hundred:\n$142, 773\\begin{vmatrix}\\div & 398 \\\\ ↓ & ↓ \\\\ 142, 800 & \\div\\end{vmatrix}400$\n\nSo we need to find the value of $142,800 \\div 400$, which we can write as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{142,800}{400}\n$$\n\nDividing the numerator and denominator by $100$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{142,800 \\div 100}{400 \\div 100} = \\dfrac{1,428}{4}\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as $1,428 \\div 4$.\n\nComputing the quotient, we obtain:\n\n| $\\,\\, {\\:\\phantom{\\mid} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$4$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!$$1$$2$$2$$2$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$2$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$8$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, $142,773 \\div 398$ is approximately $357$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138273 1069 "e-2187" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138273 1071 "Find the quotient and the remainder of $504 \\div 25$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138273 1072 #uuid "8a7c70af-22b6-5f0d-b372-01369f68b0b1" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138273 1074 "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $25$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds and tens. Notice that $25$ goes ${\\color{blue}2}$ times into ${\\color{red}50}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}2$$25$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}5} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$0$$\\color{lightgray}\\,\\downarrow$$0$$\\color{lightgray}4$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}2$Multiply: $25 \\times {\\color{blue}2} = 50$Subtract: ${\\color{red}50} - 50 = 0$Bring down: $4$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $25$ goes ${\\color{blue}0}$ times into ${\\color{red}4}$. So, ${\\color{red}4}$ is the remainder and we get:\n\n| $2$$\\color{blue}0$$25$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$0$$0$$\\color{red}4$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $504$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n504 \\div 25 = 20\\,\\text{R}\\,4 \\,\n$$\n\nSo the quotient is $20$ and the remainder is $4$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138274 1069 "e-2188" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138274 1071 "From a large container, a store owner is pouring $864\\, \\text{fl oz}$ of olive oil into several $24\\, \\text{fl oz}$ bottles. How many bottles will he need to bottle all the oil?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138274 1072 #uuid "819b56a9-999c-55f4-999e-45c58eb541d5" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138274 1074 "To find out how many bottles the store owner will use, we need to divide $864$ by $24$.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $24$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds and tens. Notice that $24$ goes ${\\color{blue}3}$ times into ${\\color{red}86}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}3$$24$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}6} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$\\color{lightgray}\\,\\downarrow$$1$$4$$\\color{lightgray}4$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}3$Multiply: $24 \\times {\\color{blue}3} = 72$Subtract: ${\\color{red}86} - 72 = 14$Bring down: $4$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $24$ goes ${\\color{blue}6}$ times into ${\\color{red}144}$. So, we get:\n\n| $3$$\\color{blue}6$$24$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}4$$\\color{red}4$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$1$$4$$4$$\\phantom{0}$$0$ | Write over ones: $\\color{blue}6$Multiply: $24 \\times {\\color{blue}6} = 144$Subtract: ${\\color{red}144} - 144 = 0$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nWe've gone through all the digits in the number $864$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n864 \\div 24 = 36\n$$\n\nSo the store owner will need a total of $36$ bottles to bottle all the olive oil." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138275 1069 "e-2189" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138275 1071 "What is the **remainder** of $987 \\div 17$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138275 1072 #uuid "db2676f8-1fa2-5225-9c9e-d5353bde01d1" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138275 1074 "We start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n| $17$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$ |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nNext, we consider the hundreds and tens. Notice that $17$ goes ${\\color{blue}5}$ times into ${\\color{red}98}$. So, we get:\n\n| $\\color{blue}5$$17$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{{\\color{red}9} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} {\\color{red}8} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$5$$\\color{lightgray}\\,\\downarrow$$1$$3$$\\color{lightgray}7$ | Write over tens: $\\color{blue}5$Multiply: $17 \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 85$Subtract: ${\\color{red}98} - 85 = 13$Bring down: $7$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n\nFinally, notice that $17$ goes ${\\color{blue}8}$ times into ${\\color{red}137}$. So, we get:\n\n| $5$$\\color{blue}8$$17$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 7}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$8$$5$$\\color{red}1$$\\color{red}3$