{longdiv}{5 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process, as usual, we get:\n\n| $0$$\\!. 4$$6$$13$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{5 {\\,. \\,} 9 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$5$$2$$7$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$8$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n5.98 \\div 13 = 0.46\\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138286 1069 "e-2255" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138286 1071 "Calculate $0.24 \\div 20$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138286 1072 #uuid "54377f4d-6e19-5134-9bcd-e67cf91de9ca" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138286 1074 "First, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $20$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{0 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\! {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$20\\,$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{0 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process, as usual, we get:\n\n| $0$$\\!\\!. 0$$1$$2$$20\\:$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{0 {\\,. \\,} 2 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$2$$0$$4$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.24 \\div 20 = 0.012\\,\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138287 1069 "e-2256" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138287 1071 "Dave was paid $\\[math]7.47$ for $1.2$ hours of work. What was Dave's hourly rate?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138287 1072 #uuid "797a90aa-e0eb-54a5-b12f-fd10185fb834" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138287 1074 "To determine Dave's hourly rate, we have to calculate\n\n$$\n7.47 \\div 1.2\n$$\n\nWe can write this division problem as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7.47}{1.2}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $10$, we get a whole number in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7.47 \\times 10}{1.2 \\times 10} = \\dfrac{74.7}{12}\n$$\n\nSo the problem is equivalent to $74.7 \\div 12$.\n\nNext, we write down the problem using long division notation, keeping the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $12$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 7}$ |\n| --- |\n\nWe also bring the decimal point up into the quotient, placing it directly above the decimal point in the dividend:\n\n| $\\:\\:\\: \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\phantom{0} {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}$$12$$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{\\color{blue}.}\\,} 7}$ |\n| --- |\n\nNow, сarrying out the long division process, as usual, we get:\n\n| $6$$\\!\\!. 2$$2$$5$$12$$\\!\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 4 {\\,. \\,} 7 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$7$$2$$2$$7$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$4$$3$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$2$$4$$6$$0$$-\\!\\!\\!$$6$$0$$0$ |\n| --- |\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n7.47 \\div 1.2 = 6.225\n$$\n\nTherefore, Dave was paid $\\[math]6.225$ per hour." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138288 1069 "e-2565" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138288 1071 "Let $C$, $D$ and $E$ be three collinear points such that $D$ is between $C$ and $E$. If $CE = 8\\,\\text{cm}$ and $CD = 6\\,\\text{cm}$, find the length of $\\overline{DE}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138288 1072 #uuid "5b1ee9ef-da39-5f7a-abb5-98149a765b1f" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138288 1074 "![](images/30/304a0de1777efb414a2504e22d53c1d15a5a0299196a7a9103a02491ff12e4c6.png)\n\nThe segment addition postulate states that\n\n$$\nCE = CD + DE\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the formula above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCE &= CD + DE \\\\\n8 &= 6 + DE \\\\\n8 - 6 &= DE \\\\\n2 &= DE\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nDE = 2 \\, \\text{cm}\n$$\n\n." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138289 1069 "e-2566" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138289 1071 "Given the diagram shown below, what is the measure of $\\overline{BC}$?\n\n![](images/54/543c7c26c7d56d8ff10f4ece71b74bb5f04edf59fddb6f2c0df549848000abaf.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138289 1072 #uuid "cabd7daa-4f22-56ca-8b26-0791857e8eae" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138289 1074 "The segment addition postulate can be applied to a set of more than three collinear points. In the given situation, it states that\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BC +CD\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the equation above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAD &= AB + BC + CD \\\\\n21 &= 5 + BC + 12 \\\\\n21 &= 17 + BC \\\\\n21 - 17 &= BC \\\\\nBC &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138290 1069 "e-2567" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138290 1071 "Given the diagram shown below, what is the value of $x$?\n\n![](images/3f/3fe1e3d1e6f89af1050d0437379a811eabb19212be36f0fecd91ddfaca2594ee.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138290 1072 #uuid "6df54266-d6d6-54f0-8b61-4b5eabf0fcbc" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138290 1074 "By the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BC +CD\n$$\n\nTo solve for $x$, we can substitute the given information\n\n$$\nAD=30, \\quad AB=x+5, \\quad BC=13, \\quad CD=5x\n$$\n\nand then solve the resulting equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAD &= AB + BC + CD \\\\\n30 &= (x + 5) + 13 + 5x \\\\\n30 &= 6x + 18 \\\\\n6x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138291 1069 "e-2568" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138291 1071 "In the diagram below, the length of the line segment $\\overline{AD}$ is $8d+12$, $AB = 4d+4$, and $C$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{BD}$. Express the length of $\\overline{AC}$ in terms of $d$.\n\n![](images/46/468fd25032d1a36e1658331053a33652cef7ff9ce3d9c95b729d6a7b7ebf1672.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138291 1072 #uuid "fc35d08c-518a-5f03-8e5f-627d58c572d3" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138291 1074 "First, note that according to the segment addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nAD = AB + BD\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBD &= AD - AB \\\\\n&= (8d + 12) - (4d + 4) \\\\\n&= 4d + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $C$ is the midpoint of $BD$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC &= \\frac{1}{2}BD \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(4d + 8) \\\\\n&= 2d + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAC &= AB + BC \\\\\n&= (4d + 4) + (2d + 4) \\\\\n&= 6d + 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138292 1069 "e-2882" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138292 1071 "Reflections across which lines carry the polygon below onto itself?\n\n![](images/c4/c4685c53819aa6a571b43ce589c798728c0e613faa3c62d5be3364fb565b99f1.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138292 1072 #uuid "ff679fb4-734f-5ed9-8295-a46253523012" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138292 1074 "Let's consider each of the lines in turn.\n\n- By reflecting the polygon across line I, we do *not* map the polygon onto itself.![](images/cd/cdc2cb0f6edaf1b63a11299b91f010977b339d99267ac78a81937cc0cdcbd38a.png)\n\n- By reflecting the polygon across line II, we map the polygon onto itself.![](images/02/023790fbfaba06e3205bd726276fe7666d48aeb97c28de6c337146f2af89f013.png)\n\n- By reflecting the polygon across line III, we do *not* map the polygon onto itself.![](images/83/83981d2603553a0f165d7773063d049ea20ae2e3ef219f9afb189a912569d9e5.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138293 1069 "e-2890" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138293 1071 "![](images/8e/8eb16eb3f40dccdc24d865553c47b4371d107c326293be62ed9441d6542f5afc.png)\n\nThe square shown above is rotated about its center $O$. Which of the following angles of rotation would carry the rotated square onto itself?\n\n1. $180^\\circ$\n2. $160^\\circ$\n3. $270^\\circ$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138293 1072 #uuid "0d0e4f13-37a5-535e-a39e-34b0d586f781" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138293 1074 "If we rotate a square about its center by multiples of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{4} = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe get the same square.\n\n![](images/67/67dc78ef691bb2a13cd6d6bc0dc7909b792d4302ec46699ce063a8301fd8ce2d.png)\n\nAmong the given options, the multiples of $90^\\circ$ are\n\n- $2 \\cdot 90^\\circ = 180^\\circ$, and\n- $3 \\cdot 90^\\circ = 270^\\circ$.\n\nTherefore, a rotation by an angle of $180^\\circ$ or $270^\\circ$ will carry the rotated square onto itself.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138294 1069 "e-2891" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138294 1071 "Reflections across which of the following lines carry the polygon below onto itself?\n\n1. the line $l$\n2. the line $y=-x$\n3. the $y$-axis\n\n![](images/2c/2c7054838587a2c29dbf8db43004c55ae66d490e99d0443bd1b0dede1755f9c5.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138294 1072 #uuid "f6b53f72-872f-5702-a599-64defa43ba8d" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138294 1074 "Let's consider each of the lines in turn.\n\n- By reflecting the polygon across $l$, we do *not* map the polygon onto itself.![](images/15/15cb44218ca154b35c8d48f4e97000c97d3c26f8e6d41726d614bc48456f1a8d.png)\n\n- By reflecting the polygon across $y=-x$, we map the polygon onto itself.![](images/0e/0e77902c4e23df35bf8fe179bbaaeda0cdc25b3f348b0cae7b01fb6c143b9f22.png)\n\n- By reflecting the polygon across $y$-axis, we map the polygon onto itself.![](images/53/5326c2eeeb304d96e24228383c19e9acfbe8a9a80713d8a96bd29d8c7f205580.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138295 1069 "e-2892" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138295 1071 "What is the order of rotational symmetry of the regular pentagon below?\n\n![](images/c2/c2ddf68523ff2a5c31d08c93f209c9feaece30e1e896bb5d6fc6cc2045b7df88.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138295 1072 #uuid "e114ece2-c082-5bfd-9df1-678aa5c6b78e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138295 1074 "If we rotate the shape about its center by multiples of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{360^\\circ}{\\color{blue}5} = 72^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe get the same shape.\nAnd $72^\\circ$ is the smallest such angle.\n\n![](images/68/681c33d303cbb1e928facaf237092eb3684d8df32e633dad6f7baec400021037.png)\n\nTherefore, the order of rotational symmetry for the given shape is ${\\color{blue}5}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138296 1069 "e-2895" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138296 1071 "How many axes of symmetry does the polygon below have?\n\n![](images/4b/4b8097b1f7c16c8761d47009ba5c7485a0a7db239d6523495ce72ca99e3d8ef4.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138296 1072 #uuid "9cf3182f-fd5c-5be1-93b3-f184ecdef2a1" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138296 1074 "The polygon has $2$ axes of symmetry, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf51e5e5a9a7196de9b53264ebd0001ea4305a3c7b17a621c8a36c3d065f6d8b.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138297 1069 "e-2898" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138297 1071 "The polygon below has rotational symmetry. Which of the marked points is the center of rotation?\n\n![](images/a0/a069fe75c9f22d2c76839eea16d354f03dad20cbb6b501943fbf29184dbb7ecb.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138297 1072 #uuid "52f3ede5-8c76-5e4d-9708-177e3d19339b" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138297 1074 "Notice that by rotating the given polygon $180^\\circ$ counterclockwise (or clockwise) about the point $C$, we map the polygon onto itself.\n\nTherefore, point $C$ is the center of rotation." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138298 1069 "e-2899" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138298 1071 "How many axes of symmetry does a regular hexagon have?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138298 1072 #uuid "e1db6c5d-670b-5dec-8485-be9bdc1d35ad" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138298 1074 "A regular hexagon has $6$ axes of symmetry, as illustrated below.\n\n![](images/f1/f1c3d4051c4e14982c623f589e4855c9da50f6751c67caeaca8fd7749603ecf2.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138299 1069 "e-3030" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138299 1071 "What is $0.14$ written in words?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138299 1072 #uuid "af1db136-bfe8-5f54-94aa-02c7118820d0" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138299 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values:\n\n| **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}0$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The whole part of the number is ${\\color{red}0}$. So, we ignore it.\n- The decimal part of the number is $\\color{blue}14$ (*fourteen*), and the rightmost digit sits at the *hundredths* place. So, we write it as *fourteen hundredths*.\n\nTherefore, $0.14$ is *fourteen hundredths*." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138300 1069 "e-3032" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138300 1071 "What is $27.05$ written in words?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138300 1072 #uuid "1d404735-be3d-5415-8d5a-bf0db696f86d" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138300 1074 "Let's start by writing down the place values:\n\n| **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}7$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}0$ | $\\color{blue}5$ |\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The whole part of the number is ${\\color{red}27}$. So, we write it as *twenty-seven*.\n- The decimal part of the number is $\\color{blue}05$ or just $\\color{blue}5$ (*five*), and the rightmost digit sits at the *hundredths* place. So, we write it as *five hundredths*.\n\nTherefore, $27.05$ expressed in words is *twenty-seven and five hundredths*." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138301 1069 "e-3033" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138301 1071 "What is \"sixty-three hundredths\" written as a decimal?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138301 1072 #uuid "8a444b44-99c0-5a6c-b79b-212a4f9332fb" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138301 1074 "We have the following:\n\n- The whole number part is absent. We write this in a place value chart as follows:\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | $\\color{red}0$ | **.** |  |  |  |\n\n- The decimal part of the number is *sixty-three hundredths*, or $\\color{blue}63$ *hundredths*. The key word here is **hundredths**. So, the rightmost digit ($3$) must sit at the *hundredths* place, as shown below.\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  |  | $\\color{red}0$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}6$ | $\\color{blue}3$ |  |\n\nTherefore, *sixty-three hundredths* is $0.63$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138302 1069 "e-3034" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138302 1071 "What is \"two and ninety-four thousandths\" written as a decimal?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138302 1072 #uuid "e60cf077-5808-5d58-a8f1-c48ce9887f03" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138302 1074 "We have the following:\n\n- The whole part is *two* $({\\color{red}2})$. We write this in a place value chart as follows:\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $\\color{red}$ | $\\color{red}2$ | **.** |  |  |  |\n\n- The decimal part of the number is *ninety-four thousandths* or $\\color{blue}94$ *thousandths*. The key word here is **thousandths**. So, the rightmost digit ($4$) must sit at the *thousandths* place, as shown below.\n\n| **hundreds** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** | **thousandths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $\\color{red}$ | $\\color{red}2$ | **.** | $\\color{blue}0$ | $\\color{blue}9$ | $\\color{blue}4$ |\n\nNotice that the cell corresponding to the *tenths* place is filled with a zero.\n\nTherefore, *two and ninety-four thousandths* is $2.094$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138303 1069 "e-4360" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138303 1071 "Factor $x^4-4x^2+4$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138303 1072 #uuid "15bbccd7-d0ce-50bb-80bb-777b5d0a7245" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138303 1074 "We can introduce a new variable $z=x^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{4} - 4x^{2} + 4 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2})^{2} - 4(x^{2}) + 4 &=  \\\\\n&= z^{2} - 4z + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic as the square of a difference, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{2} - 4z + 4 &=  \\\\\n(z - 2)^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138304 1069 "e-4362" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138304 1071 "Factor the expression $x^3 - 4x$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138304 1072 #uuid "ebc0888c-bc8b-5f8c-8e2a-667ca1490d37" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138304 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $x$. To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 4x = x(x^2 - 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2 - 4$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\nx(x^2 - 4) = x(x+2)(x-2)\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138305 1069 "e-4778" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138305 1071 "Express $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{7}}$ as the quotient of two radicals." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138305 1072 #uuid "571f96ea-2c3c-55e6-b776-9009301d8d9e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138305 1074 "Applying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{7}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{7}}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138306 1069 "e-4779" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138306 1071 "Express $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{12}}{\\sqrt{3}}$ as a single radical." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138306 1072 #uuid "96cf1739-aa80-574d-900e-3ff75517cfe9" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138306 1074 "Using the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{12}}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{12}{3}}= \\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138307 1069 "e-4780" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138307 1071 "Express $\\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{3}}{\\sqrt[3]{4}}$ as a single radical." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138307 1072 #uuid "f0bfcf6d-08a2-5dc4-b66c-814445c05e76" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138307 1074 "Applying the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt[3]{3}}{\\sqrt[3]{4}} = \\sqrt[3] {\\dfrac{3}{4}}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138308 1069 "e-4999" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138308 1071 "Factor the expression $x^3 - 6x^2 + 9x$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138308 1072 #uuid "da0223c6-ff03-55da-992c-d8a1ef3f9aff" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138308 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $x$. To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 6x^2 + 9x = x(x^2 - 6x + 9)\n$$\n\nThe expression $x^2 - 6x + 9$ is a perfect square trinomial that can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x^{2} - 6x + 9) &=  \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 6x + 3^{2}) &=  \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 3x + 3^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= x(x - 3)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138309 1069 "e-5000" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138309 1071 "Factor the expression $x^3 + x^2 - 2x$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138309 1072 #uuid "007b9b7b-5fab-5e50-9598-fc94a9f6edde" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138309 1074 "Note that all of the terms have a common factor of $x$. To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} + x^{2} - 2x &= x(x^{2} + x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the polynomial $x^2 + x - 2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x^{2} + x - 2) &=  \\\\\nx((x^{2} - x) + (2x - 2)) &=  \\\\\nx(x(x - 1) + 2(x - 1)) &=  \\\\\n&= x(x + 2)(x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138310 1069 "e-5018" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138310 1071 "Factor $t^4 - t^2 - 2$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138310 1072 #uuid "12f9f52c-0baa-557b-83b7-d80c8a34c409" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138310 1074 "We can introduce a new variable $z = t^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{4} - t^{2} - 2 &=  \\\\\n(t^{2})^{2} - t^{2} - 2 &=  \\\\\n&= z^{2} - z - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^{2} - z - 2 &=  \\\\\n(z + 1)(z - 2) &=  \\\\\n&= (t^{2} + 1)(t^{2} - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138311 1069 "e-5019" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138311 1071 "Factor $2p^4 + 5p^2 + 3$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138311 1072 #uuid "f503abb5-10a5-56bf-b07b-7ebe1ed04049" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138311 1074 "We can introduce a new variable $t = p^2$ and then rewrite the trinomial as a quadratic in terms of $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2p^{4} + 5p^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n2(p^{2})^{2} + 5p^{2} + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= 2t^{2} + 5t + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the quadratic, and then rewrite the final expression in terms of $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2t^{2} + 5t + 3 &=  \\\\\n(t + 1)(2t + 3) &=  \\\\\n&= (p^{2} + 1)(2p^{2} + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138312 1069 "e-5198" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138312 1071 "Consider the following functions:\n\n$f(x)=8-x, \\qquad g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{50x}$\n\nBy filling the empty cells in the table below, find an approximate solution to $f(x)=g(x)$.\n\n| $x$ | $2.5$ | $2.6$ | $2.7$ | $2.8$ | $2.9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ |  | $5.4$ |  |  | $5.1$ |\n| $g(x)$ |  | $5.07$ |  |  | $5.25$ |\n| $f(x) - g(x)$ |  | $0.33$ |  |  | $-0.15$ |\n| $\\mid f(x)-g(x) \\mid$ |  | $0.33$ |  |  | $0.15$ |" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138312 1072 #uuid "ac7d3e5b-9241-5bf4-bc27-c8b6d054f245" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138312 1074 "We fill in the table and get the following result:\n\n| $x$ | $2.5$ | $2.6$ | $2.7$ | $2.8$ | $2.9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $5.5$ | $5.4$ | $5.3$ | $5.2$ | $5.1$ |\n| $g(x)$ | $5.00$ | $5.07$ | $5.13$ | $5.19$ | $5.25$ |\n| $f(x) - g(x)$ | $0.50$ | $0.33$ | $0.17$ | $0.01$ | $-0.15$ |\n| $\\mid f(x)-g(x) \\mid$ | $0.50$ | $0.33$ | $0.17$ | $0.01$ | $0.15$ |\n\nThe expression $\\mid f(x)-g(x) \\mid$ gives the error. The smallest error is $0.01$, given by the value $x=2.8$.\n\nTherefore, among the given options, the best approximation to the solution is $x=2.8$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138313 1069 "e-5199" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138313 1071 "![](images/d1/d1e168bdbd8d02a5cf4b18bbeb6cd5a30aa2089e7a3dca274b5ef4c53efc2b74.png)\n\nUsing the graph, find an approximate solution to the system of equations below.\n\n${y + x^{3} = 4; y = 2x^{2} - 1$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138313 1072 #uuid "d1fcb9f2-db20-5faa-9285-f97c870f122e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138313 1074 "The solution to the system of equations corresponds to the point of intersection of the two curves.\n\n![](images/a4/a4bac1511ac1e32112c51f455985909be74fa8254f494bc32566789cbf1f0fb7.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\nx \\approx 1.2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\approx 2.1\n$$\n\nis an approximate solution." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138314 1069 "e-5202" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138314 1071 "![](images/8f/8f43c98b96ea14f0224b72dc31ba1583ee24b6671d5f2d2afc122ac2a4b23dd6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are defined as\n$f(x) = x-2, \\qquad g(x) = \\dfrac{2x}{x^2 + 1}$.\n\nBy creating a table of values, find, to one decimal place, a solution to the equation $f(x) = g(x)$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138314 1072 #uuid "0642c21f-38b0-5b37-8a53-1eb995840038" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138314 1074 "![](images/6a/6aec4d128d8870609e46d28ade9a593b21756cda6e2492da3d2cce3a15fdb4d3.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we identify that the point of intersection lies between $x=2.6$ and $x=2.8$. So, we create a table of values as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $2.6$ | $2.7$ | $2.8$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.6$ | $0.7$ | $0.8$ |\n| $g(x)$ | $0.67$ | $0.65$ | $0.63$ |\n| $f(x) - g(x)$ | $-0.07$ | $0.05$ | $0.17$ |\n| $\\mid f(x) - g(x) \\mid$ | $0.07$ | $0.05$ | $0.17$ |\n\nIt appears from the table that the solution to\n\n$$\nf(x) = g(x)\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nx \\approx 2.7\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nWe can verify that this the case by computing $f(x) - g(x)$ for the upper and lower bounds of\n\n$$\nx\\approx 2.7\n$$\n\nand checking for a change in sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(2.65) - g(2.65) &= (2.65 - 2) - (2(2.65))/((2.65)^{2} + 1) \\\\\n&\\approx - 0.01 < 0 \\\\\nf(2.75) - g(2.75) &= (2.75 - 2) - (2(2.75))/((2.75)^{2} + 1) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.11 > 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there is a change in sign, we can conclude that\n\n$$\nx \\approx 2.7\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138315 1069 "e-8128" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138315 1071 "What is the missing number in the following equality?\n\n$562.01 = 5 \\times 100 + 6 \\times 10 + 2 \\times 1 + \\underline{\\phantom{{}^{55555555555}}}$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138315 1072 #uuid "cc168c9d-4b3d-5afa-8aa1-869aed774288" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138315 1074 "We need to convert the given number from standard form to expanded form.\n\nLet's start by writing down the place values of $562.01$:\n\n| **hundredts** | **tens** | **ones** |  | **tenths** | **hundredths** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $6$ | $2$ | **.** | $0$ | $1$ |\n\n$\\qquad$ $5$ hundreds is $500$, which we can write as $5 \\times 100$\n\n$\\qquad$ $6$ tens is $60$, which we can write as $6 \\times 10$\n\n$\\qquad$ $2$ ones is $2$, which we can write as $2 \\times 1$\n\n$\\qquad$ $0$ tenths is $0$\n\n$\\qquad$ $1$ hundredth is $0.01$, which we can write as $1 \\times 0.01$\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n562.01 = 5 \\times 100 + 6 \\times 10 + 2 \\times 1 + 1\\times 0.01\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $1 \\times 0.01$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138316 1069 "e-896" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138316 1071 "The gravitational acceleration $g$ of a body in free fall is given by the formula\n$g = \\dfrac{2h}{t^2}$,\nwhere $h$ is the distance, measured in meters, traveled by the body due to gravity in time $t$, measured in seconds.\nDetermine an appropriate measurement unit for gravitational acceleration." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138316 1072 #uuid "36127b89-97ba-5f98-bd8e-b36ae1a261f4" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138316 1074 "To find the unit of gravitational acceleration, we substitute the given units of distance and time into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng &= \\frac{2h}{t^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot m}{s^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{m}{s^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the appropriate unit is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\n$$\n\nHere, we ignore the unitless constant $2$ since it does not affect the units." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138317 1069 "e-897" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138317 1071 "The frequency of a wave is given by the formula\n$f = \\dfrac{v}{l}$,\nwhere $v$ represents the velocity of the wave in $\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}$, while $l$ represents the length of the wave in meters. Find an appropriate measurement unit for frequency." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138317 1072 #uuid "ee3fa65e-1146-598a-a89d-33d287e88066" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138317 1074 "We can find the unit of frequency by substituting the given units of velocity and length into the formula and simplifying.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf &= \\frac{v}{l} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{m}{s}))/(m) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{m} \\cdot (\\frac{m}{s}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{m}{m \\cdot s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{m}{m \\cdot s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of frequency is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138318 1069 "e-898" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138318 1071 "A farm with $115$ chickens produces $20\\,000$ eggs per year. To determine the number of eggs $P$ produced by one chicken on average, the farmer uses the formula\n$P=\\dfrac{N}{C}$,\nwhere $N$ is the number of eggs produced by the farm during the year, and $C$ is the number of chickens on the farm.\n\nIn words, what is the appropriate measurement unit for $P$?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138318 1072 #uuid "b0212d4c-eb7a-5cec-8eb1-1c60be355f4e" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138318 1074 "We are solving for measurement units, so we can ignore the quantities $115$ and $20\\,000$. They do not matter if we are just solving for units.\n\nSince $N$ represents $\\textrm{eggs}$ and $C$ represents $\\textrm{chickens}$, the appropriate measurement unit for\n\n$$\nP=\\dfrac{N}{C}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{eggs}}{\\textrm{chickens}}\n$$\n\nIn words, this is \"eggs per chicken.\"" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138319 1069 "e-901" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138319 1071 "A tank is being filled with water. What is an appropriate unit for measuring the rate of change of water volume in the tank, as time goes on?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138319 1072 #uuid "66c31f3c-2abb-51ff-b8d5-8377aeb90d80" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138319 1074 "We *measure the change* of the volume as *we change* the amount of time. Consequently, any appropriate unit for measuring the rate of change of water volume in the tank must take the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{unit of volume}}{\\textrm{unit of time}}\n$$\n\nSome examples of appropriate units include\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{liters}}{\\textrm{hour}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^3}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{cm}^3}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138320 1069 "e-907" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138320 1071 "A guitar string, made of carbon steel, has a linear mass density of $3\\,950$ milligrams per meter. What is the linear mass density in grams per meter?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138320 1072 #uuid "638af342-1995-5403-84bd-b6c720cb432d" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138320 1074 "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nTo figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot\\, \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\textrm{mg}$ with $\\textrm{g}$. To get rid of $\\textrm{mg}$, we can put $\\textrm{mg}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\textrm{g}$, we can put $\\textrm{g}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{mg}}}{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{mg}}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{mg}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The density given in the problem statement is $3\\,950 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}}$.\n- There are $1\\,000$ milligrams per gram. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}$ grams per milligram.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3950\\frac{mg}{m} &= 3950\\frac{mg}{m} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1000}\\frac{g}{mg} \\\\\n&= (3950 \\cdot \\frac{1}{1000})\\frac{mg}{m} \\cdot \\frac{g}{mg} \\\\\n&= 3.95\\frac{g}{m}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the linear mass density of the string is $3.95\\,\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{m}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138321 1069 "e-908" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138321 1071 "Sand runs through a sand timer at a rate of $120$ grams per minute. What is this rate in grams per second?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138321 1072 #uuid "08aa3547-cb51-5d00-8cb7-3e1a9bd4a695" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138321 1074 "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nTo figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}}\\cdot\\, \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\textrm{min}$ with $\\textrm{s}$. To get rid of $\\textrm{min}$, we can put $\\textrm{min}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\textrm{s}$, we can put $\\textrm{s}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{min}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{min}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{min}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $120 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}}$.\n- There are $60$ seconds per minute. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ minutes per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n120\\frac{g}{min} &= 120\\frac{g}{min} \\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\frac{min}{s} \\\\\n&= (120 \\cdot \\frac{1}{60})\\frac{g}{min} \\cdot \\frac{min}{s} \\\\\n&= 2\\frac{g}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the sand runs through the sand timer at a rate of $2 \\,\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{s}$." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138322 1069 "e-909" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138322 1071 "A peregrine falcon can reach a speed of $360$ kilometers per hour during flight while diving. What is this speed in meters per second?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138322 1072 #uuid "79f9c66b-2e4e-53ad-8146-10a2aa4e730d" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138322 1074 "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{hour}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{hour}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}$ to $\\textrm{m}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}}}{\\textrm{hour}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{hour}$ into $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}}}{\\cancel{\\color{red}\\textrm{hour}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\color{red}\\textrm{hour}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{black}\\textrm{km}}{\\color{black}\\textrm{hour}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\color{black}\\textrm{km}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\color{black}\\textrm{hour}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $360 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{hour}}$.\n- There are $1\\,000$ meters per kilometer.\n- The number of seconds in one hour is given by\n$1hour|= 60minutes; = 60 \\cdot 1minute; = 60 \\cdot 60s; = 3600s$.\nTaking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3\\,600}$ hours per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n360\\frac{km}{hour} &= 360\\frac{km}{hour} \\cdot 1000\\frac{m}{km} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3600}\\frac{hour}{s} \\\\\n&= (360 \\cdot 1000 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3600})\\frac{km}{hour} \\cdot \\frac{m}{km} \\cdot \\frac{hour}{s} \\\\\n&= 100\\frac{m}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the speed of the falcon is $100$ meters per second." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138323 1069 "e-9407" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138323 1071 "Matt competes in a go-karting race. The amount of fuel in his car decreases by a fixed amount on each lap. There are $23$ liters of fuel remaining after the third lap and $11$ liters remaining after the seventh lap. How much fuel does Matt's car consume per lap?" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138323 1072 #uuid "7750d1b8-2de5-52e8-9556-82fe77572ab7" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138323 1074 "Since the amount of fuel in the car *decreases* at the same rate each lap, the amount of fuel remaining in Matt's car can be modeled as an arithmetic sequence.\n\nWe are given the following values:\n\n$$\na_{3} = 23, a_{7} = 11\n$$\n\nTo compute the common difference, we divide the total decrease by the number of jumps between the $3$rd and $7$th laps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{a_{n} - a_{m}}{n - m} \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{3} - a_{7}}{3 - 7} \\\\\n&= \\frac{23 - 11}{3 - 7} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{-4} \\\\\n&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Matt's car consumes $3$ liters of fuel per lap." 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138324 1069 "e-9802" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138324 1071 "The complex number $z$ is shown on the argand diagram below. Sketch a diagram that shows the complex conjugate of $z$.\n\n![](images/bf/bfd1768972ea0314c078a5d368ba7ecb146a3c1f6365d8da2c8e7e1b031ce528.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138324 1072 #uuid "a6895526-cac9-58db-b55c-81666e59c2ff" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138324 1074 "The conjugate of\n\n$$\nz=x + \\textrm{i} y\n$$\n\ndenoted $\\bar{z}$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\bar{z} = x - \\textrm{i} y\n$$\n\nTo plot $\\overline{z}$ on an Argand diagram, we reflect $z$ across the real axis, as illustrated below.\n\n![](images/16/16b82513d305562cb80b6f4feb386d027ef009bd8b4d7d809ae58529441dc130.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138325 1069 "e-9803" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138325 1071 "The complex numbers $z_1$ and $z_2$ are shown on the argand diagram below. Sketch a diagram that shows the complex number $z_3 = z_2 - z_1$.\n\n![](images/8e/8e51ee9517fca0af487a240bb039eeabc2e02ec0dd689d11cc08464584673163.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138325 1072 #uuid "956959a1-1c63-5346-b824-3d5bb4eb1a6a" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138325 1074 "Recall that the difference $z_2 - z_1$ is equivalent to the sum $z_2 + (-z_1)$.\n\nFirst, we plot the complex number $(-z_1){:}$\n\n![](images/01/0129d229e970fe8d4fcd8c68c26cd445d84d9f7877ad841a272a73e8de422290.png)\n\nNow, we add the vectors corresponding to $z_2$ and $(-z_1)$ using the parallelogram law, as shown below:\n\n![](images/ce/ce954fd5d912ef0d79d18dbe6941e8f57289fcfc36c6a2c2dbf8431ae0349a4a.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138326 1069 "e-9804" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138326 1071 "The complex numbers $z_1$ and $z_2$ are shown on the argand diagram below. Sketch a diagram that shows the complex number $z_3 = z_1 \\cdot z_2$.\n\n![](images/f8/f89642a7f9a3363953e7003d38b6e2849311ab4de9a4088350ca132528c5083b.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138326 1072 #uuid "64cc6ac9-aefb-5b66-ae9f-e27eb6d30f95" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138326 1074 "First, we calculate the modulus of $z_3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}z_3 &= & z_1 & \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}z_2|=4 \\cdot 1.5 = 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the arguments of $z_1$ and $z_2$ are equal to\n\n$$\n\\theta_1=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta_2=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nrespectively. So, the argument of $z_3$ equals\n\n$$\n\\theta_3 = \\theta_1 + \\theta_2 = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} + \\dfrac{5\\pi}{4} = 2\\pi\n$$\n\nThe diagram below shows the resulting complex number.\n\n![](images/97/977f8d80c5a7bcf7b84c9125b83f63cc9e3cdc3efd1dd33a33c2050b4a4b2878.png)" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138327 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048415 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186139057 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186139063 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138327 1039 "Writing a Decimal With No Whole Number Part in Words" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138327 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138299 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138327 1042 #uuid "d6ce2884-e2f2-5fa6-a7d6-17df3154d07c" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186139064 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186139068 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138328 1039 "Writing a Decimal With a Whole Number Part in Words" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138328 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138300 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138328 1042 #uuid "bc432209-443f-537d-ba4a-a3096f4c0e5a" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138329 1038 #edb/ref 17592186139058 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138329 1038 #edb/ref 17592186139069 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138329 1039 "Reading a Decimal With No Whole Number Part Written in Words" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138329 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138301 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138329 1042 #uuid "95cab323-ab65-5cba-9fa7-7b1e44446236" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138330 1038 #edb/ref 17592186139070 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138330 1038 #edb/ref 17592186139072 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138330 1039 "Reading a Decimal With a Whole Number Part Written in Words" 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [17592186138330 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138302 13194139533361 17592186045585 true] [175